(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["correction-log-2026-10-22"]={"html":"<h1>Correction log — 22 October 2026 (v1.3.4)</h1>\n<p>Changes made in v1.3.4 to text signed at v1.3.3. This version corrects citations against their sources. Where a canvas reads more into a regulatory instrument than the instrument states, and section 1.2 lists that reading, the canvas now says that the reading is its own. The version corrects statements about the standard that the standard's own text contradicts, among them one reviewer-day figure. It gives incident register rows their markers after a new reading of their sources, adds rows 92 to 101 and cites nine of them from the canvases. It adds one entry to the regulatory source register. It names two further method classes in <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3, and it adds one grading rule for all procedures in <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-09.md. An entry that rests on a published source names that source; the other entries rest on the owner's decisions and on the standard's own text. A sentence that names a row or a line and the kind of change declares that change; the row carries the new values. The change logs of the affected canvases and playbooks carry their own v1.3.4 (2026-10-22) lines, which give the detail for each canvas.</p>\n<p>No ★ rating changes. Field 11 changes in 11 canvases, and no rating moves in any of them (sections 1 and 2). <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.1 states that the ★ rating in each canvas carries its anchor, in place of every ★ rating (section 2). Part C of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> names the two tests that the register rates ★4 (section 2). The method class of three procedures changes (section 2). TP-AGT-003 reads Emerging Practice in place of Adapted Industry Practice. TP-AGT-006 and TP-TPR-005 read \"Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice\" in place of \"Mixed\". <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3 adds two entries, \"None established\" and \"Two classes\", and §1 of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> names \"None established\" and states the two-class rule. No Independent Review Question or claim is added, removed or reworded: <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code> do not change. In IRQ-SEC-014 the applicability note of field 1 changes, and the question does not (section 2). <code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code> quotes two questions in full, as the register gives them (section 2). One procedure's reviewer-day figure changes. TP-SEC-002 reads 5.5 in place of 5.0, the sum of its stated parts. The Security domain total moves from 57.0 to 57.5, and the programme total from 455.0 to 455.5 (section 2). No risk-profile value changes: no axis score moves in field 15 or field 16. In IRQ-GOV-016 field 15, one abbreviation is spelled out (section 1.1). No procedure changes its own grade rule. TP-RES-001 §6 removes from its Partial interpretation a statement about the canvas that the canvas does not make (section 2). <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2 adds one grading rule for all procedures: where the facts meet a fail indicator and a partial indicator, the result is Fail. It also states that the procedure's own grade rule decides whether a result is Partial, and that field 14 gives the reporting language and is not a grade rule (section 2). IRQ-GOV-012 field 11 adds a sixth evidence limb, limb (6), to the test, and its ★2 does not move (section 2). No under-research observation is opened or closed. URO-7 and URO-8 stay open past their signing-batch limit (section 6). Two [UNVERIFIED] tags are removed, in field 17 of IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-013, where paragraph pinpoints replace them (section 1.2). In field 17 of IRQ-GOV-008 to IRQ-GOV-012, the [UNVERIFIED] tag stays and lists only the instruments that the canvas cites (section 2). The head of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> adds a note on rows that record no verification and carry no [UNVERIFIED] mark (section 3). The release adds rows 92 to 101 to the incident register (section 4) and one entry to the regulatory source register (section 3). <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2 counts a register addition as a change to the standard's substance. Where a canvas's field 18 Research Basis changes, its \"Research basis last reviewed\" date moves to 22 October 2026. This applies to 35 canvases, among them the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003. No other canvas's date moves. IRQ-MOD-016 adds a worked example to field 10 and keeps its date (section 5). Field 10 of the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003 also changes (section 2). IRQ-SEC-002 changes one evidence item in field 10 and keeps its date (section 1.3).</p>\n<h2>1. Citations and sources</h2>\n<h3>1.1 Statements corrected to what the source says</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>McFarlane et al. (2024), <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.</strong> The precedent is stated as the paper gives it (Clinical and Public Health Guidelines 1(4), e12012: abstract, results and conclusion). Its 1,182 recommendations come from 26 NICE COVID-19 living guidelines, not WHO guidelines. The paper finds that some recommendations need an update sooner than others. It argues for a flexible, responsive approach to surveillance, set by the pace of change, in which guideline developers choose which recommendations to keep fully living. The figures of 739 and 335 days stand.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA market study MS18/1.3 (September 2020), IRQ-CON-006 field 18.</strong> The tenure figures are given in pounds, as the study states them, not as percentages (para 3.9, p.13). A motor customer with more than five years' tenure paid on average £85 more than a new customer with the same risk, against a new-business price of £285. A combined buildings-and-contents customer paid £122 more, against £165. The £1.2bn figure carries its base: 6 million policyholders who paid high prices in 2018 (para 1.6). The £4.2bn saving carries its scenario, no change in the intensity of competition (para 5.19).</li>\n<li><strong>Zeka and Johns (2026, Financial Planning Review), IRQ-CON-003 field 18.</strong> The five scenarios are listed as Table 1 of the paper gives them. Illness is not among them. The recommendations varied across tools (abstract), in place of \"conflicting recommendations\". The gender-role bias is placed in one scenario (scenario 2).</li>\n<li><strong>Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan (arXiv:1609.05807), IRQ-CON-001 fields 7 and 9.</strong> The canvas cites the paper for its own result (§1; Theorem 1.1). Calibration within groups and balance for the positive and negative classes cannot all hold at once, except with perfect prediction or equal base rates. The paper does not include demographic parity in that result.</li>\n<li><strong>MemToC (Varlamov et al., arXiv:2608.26295v1), IRQ-AGT-008 field 18 and <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong> The benchmark sets a model's own parametric memory, an elicited closed-book answer, against a tool return of known correctness (abstract; §3.3). It has no stored memory entry and no source-of-truth system. The canvas no longer says that the benchmark measures the construct of its field 11. It names the benchmark as the nearest published benchmark of memory–tool arbitration. The finding and relevance cells of the register row say the same. The full text was read to §4 on 22 October 2026. The ★1 rating is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>GSM1k (Zhang et al., arXiv:2405.00332), IRQ-MOD-011 field 7.</strong> The GSM8K example states what the re-creation of the test found: score drops in some model families (§5.1), and little or no sign of overfitting in frontier models (abstract; conclusion). It had said that frontier models were substantially contaminated.</li>\n<li><strong>Kadavath et al. (2022) is an arXiv preprint.</strong> IRQ-MOD-005 (fields 11 and 21), the ★ line of TP-MOD-005 and the field 21 footer of the exemplar canvas for IRQ-MOD-006 no longer call it peer-reviewed. In IRQ-MOD-005 the peer-reviewed limb rests on Xiong et al. (ICLR 2024) and Tian et al. (EMNLP 2023). In TP-MOD-005 it rests on Xiong et al. (ICLR 2024) and Tian et al. (EMNLP 2023), as in the canvas. In the exemplar canvas it rests on Farquhar et al. (Nature, 2024) and Guo et al. (ICML 2017).</li>\n<li><strong>Doshi-Velez and Kim (arXiv:1702.08608v2).</strong> The evaluation level that the test of IRQ-HUM-007 adapts is named application-grounded (real humans, real tasks; §3.1). It had been named human-grounded, which is the paper's level for simplified tasks (§3.2). The change is made in IRQ-HUM-007 fields 9, 11, 14 and 18, in the confidence and method-class line of TP-HUM-007, and in the TP-HUM-007 row of the domain table of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_HUM.md</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>BCBS 239 (January 2013), IRQ-GOV-016 and TP-GOV-016.</strong> BCBS 239 names no traceability principle. The canvas no longer credits it with a traceability principle, requirement or expectation (fields 9, 17 and 21). The flagging control extends the expectations of BCBS 239 on metadata about the characteristics of the data (Principle 2, para 33) and on documented aggregation processes (Principle 3, para 39). The traceability control rests on the reconciliation of risk data to the bank's sources (para 36(c)). It also rests on the data-lineage expectation of the ECB \"Guide on effective risk data aggregation and risk reporting\" (May 2024, Section 3.4), which field 17 now cites. The guide does not address AI-originated content. Field 17 names the principles of accuracy and integrity, completeness, timeliness and adaptability as Principles 3 to 6. The 2016 date states that global systemically important banks identified in 2011 or 2012 must meet the Principles by January 2016 (para 14). This replaces \"initially binding\" from 1 January 2016 (fields 9, 17 and 20, and the method classification of TP-GOV-016). RDARR is spelled out at its first use in field 15 and in the personnel section of TP-GOV-016.</li>\n<li>The change log of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_GOV.md</code> adds a v1.3.4 line for TP-GOV-016. It records the 2016 date in the method classification and RDARR in full in the personnel section. The ★2 rating and the reviewer-day estimate are unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>NIST AI RMF 1.0 (NIST AI 100-1, January 2023).</strong> IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-002 and IRQ-HUM-008 field 9, and the control example for IRQ-HUM-001 in §9 of <code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>, cite the category as MANAGE 4, as NIST AI 100-1 numbers it, in place of MANAGE-4. The control example for IRQ-DAT-007 in §9 of <code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>, and the example version record in §4 of <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code>, cite the category MEASURE 2 in the same form, in place of MEASURE-2.</li>\n<li><strong>ISAE 3000 (Revised), <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, Layer 2.</strong> The sentence on qualified conclusions keeps para 74 for a material scope limitation or a material misstatement. It adds para 43 for criteria found unsuitable, or subject matter found not appropriate, after the engagement is accepted.</li>\n<li><strong>ISAE 3000 (Revised), <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> Part A.3.</strong> The rule on a later re-rating now applies to a re-rating made because research has moved. The rule cites the treatment of subsequent events in an assurance engagement (ISAE 3000 (Revised), para 61), with ISA 560 and IAS 10 as the audit analogue. It had cited ISA 560 and IAS 10 only. A new sentence states that a rating corrected because it was wrong when the opinion was issued is not research movement. The correction log names such a rating, and the issuer considers whether the conclusion still stands.</li>\n<li><strong>Assurance case and Goal Structuring Notation.</strong> Part I.1 of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> and the Purpose of Component 6 in <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> cite two sources: ISO/IEC 15026-2 for the assurance case, and the GSN Community Standard, Version 2, for the notation. They had cited ISO/IEC 15026-2 for Goal Structuring Notation. In <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code> §2 (Design evolution), row 4 says that the practice comes from safety-critical industries. It names the GSN Community Standard, UL 4600 (for autonomous products) and ISO/IEC 15026-2, in place of aviation, nuclear and medical devices. These are the sources that the References &amp; Standards of <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> list.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.2 Regulatory instruments: what they require, and what the canvas reads into them</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PRA SS1/21 (March 2022) and FCA SYSC 15A.</strong> The operational resilience limbs state what the instruments require and expect. A firm must remain within impact tolerance in a severe but plausible disruption (SS1/21 para 4.1; SYSC 15A.2.9R). It sets its tolerances without regard to the cause of disruption (SS1/21 para 3.5). It maps the people, processes, technology, facilities and information that deliver each important business service (SS1/21 para 5.1). It tests regularly (SS1/21 para 6.1; SYSC 15A.5.3R) and acts on the vulnerabilities that mapping and testing find (SS1/21 para 5.3). SS1/21 expects that testing to focus on response and recovery, not on prevention (para 6.1). A firm's communication plans include escalation paths (SS1/21 para 4.7). Neither instrument requires safe degradation or tested escalation paths, and SS1/21 states no containment expectation. SS1/21 notes that there may be circumstances in which continuing to deliver a service through disruption has a more adverse impact than suspending it (para 3.15). FCA guidance treats resuming a degraded service as usually only appropriate where, having regard to the interest of the firm's clients, the soundness, stability and resilience of the UK financial system and the orderly operation of the financial markets, the benefits of resuming it outweigh the negatives of keeping the service unavailable until the issues have been fully remediated and the service is able to be fully restored to its pre-disruption levels (SYSC 15A.2.10G). Each canvas now states its control as its own reading of the regime:</li>\n<li>safe degradation: IRQ-AGT-005 fields 9 and 17, and IRQ-AGT-013 field 17, which also names automated processes;</li>\n<li>mid-plan termination: IRQ-AGT-002 field 9;</li>\n<li>runaway-delegation containment, and disruption from uncontrolled resource consumption: IRQ-AGT-006 fields 9 and 17;</li>\n<li>structural gating of irreversible actions: IRQ-AGT-012 field 17;</li>\n<li>tested escalation: IRQ-GOV-008 fields 9, 17 and 21.</li>\n<li>In field 17 of IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-013, the paragraph pinpoints replace an [UNVERIFIED] tag that said no paragraph pinpoint was confirmed.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008 field 5 states what PRA SS1/23 (April 2026) expects of ongoing monitoring under Principle 4.4(b)(iii). Firms assess whether changes in products, exposures, activities, clients or market conditions should be addressed through model adjustments, recalibration or redevelopment, or by the replacement of the model. The canvas states as its own reading, of that expectation and of the operational resilience regime, that operational signals must inform governance decisions. The text had said that SS1/23 and the operational resilience regime both expect this.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008 fields 9 and 17 state what SS1/23 (April 2026) expects. Under Principle 4.4(b)(iii), firms use ongoing monitoring to assess whether changes in products, exposures, activities, clients or market conditions call for model adjustment, recalibration, redevelopment or replacement. Under Principle 5.2(a), firms consider restrictions on model use when model performance tests show that a significant breach has occurred or is likely. The canvas states as its own reading that monitoring outcomes must inform governance decisions on model status, use restriction or withdrawal. The text had said that SS1/23 requires this. The verification note of field 17 records the read of Principles 4.4 and 5.2.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring, IRQ-RES-005 fields 5 and 7.</strong> No source states the quoted expectation of \"continuous monitoring with defined triggers\". Field 5 cites the PRA SS1/23 expectations on which the limb rests: firms monitor model performance against thresholds, and apply mitigants once defined triggers and thresholds are breached (SS1/23, April 2026, Principles 4.4(a) and 5.3(a)(ii)). PRA SS1/21 states no monitoring expectation. Field 5 states threshold-triggered response as the canvas's reading of SS1/21 para 4.16, which calls operational resilience a dynamic activity. Field 7 states it as the canvas's reading too.</li>\n<li><strong>PRA SS1/23 (April 2026), pinpoints.</strong></li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-007 fields 9 and 17. Principle 4 is the anchor for the independence of validation from model development and from the people who own the models, with the standing to challenge (4.1(d) and (e)). The validation function recommends to the model approval authority (4.1(b)), and that authority ensures that recommendations for remediation or redevelopment are actioned (Principle 4). SS1/23 does not give the validation function authority to reject use. Restrictions on model use are a decision of the firm under 5.2. The authority to reject deployment is stated as the canvas's extension.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-001 fields 9 and 17 cite Principle 2 (Governance) at 2.4(c), which asks that the firm document who owns each model. This replaces \"governance expectations\".</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-008 field 17 cites Principle 3.5, in place of Principles 2 and 4. It states what 3.5(a) asks: documentation that lets an independent third party replicate parameter estimation and model results. The reconstruction of decisions that depend on retrieval is the canvas's extension.</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-012 field 17 cites Principles 4.4 (model performance monitoring) and 4.3 (process verification), in place of \"Principle 4/5\". Principle 4.3(a) asks firms to confirm that all model components operate effectively. The canvas reads the retrieval layer as one of those components.</li>\n<li><strong>Model inventory, IRQ-TPR-003 fields 17 and 21.</strong> The inventory limb rests on a PRA expectation: banks keep a comprehensive model inventory that includes AI/ML models. The source is SS1/23, as the Bank of England and PRA letter of 22 April 2024 states it (p.10). The FCA AI Update (April 2024) names no inventory, and the canvas no longer attributes the expectation to it. An inventory of all AI use, however delivered, is the canvas's reading.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA AI Update (April 2024), IRQ-AGT-009 field 17.</strong> The Update notes that SYSC 4.1.1R requires robust governance arrangements (para 3.39). It states no change-control expectation. Control of the changes that affect regulated activities is the canvas's reading of SYSC 4.1.1R.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA AI Update (April 2024), IRQ-GOV-005 fields 9, 17 and 21.</strong> The Update sets out how the existing framework, including SYSC and the Consumer Duty, applies to firms' use of AI (paras 3.7 and 4.3). It prescribes no AI policy content. The translation of those obligations into firm-level AI policy is now the canvas's reading. The text had said that the FCA confirms or expects that translation. Field 21 (Test 2) states the same reading.</li>\n<li><strong>UK GDPR.</strong> IRQ-DAT-010 field 17 states what the Articles say. Art. 17(1) gives a right to erasure without undue delay, on the grounds it lists. Art. 30(1) requires a controller to keep a record of its processing activities. Verifiable deletion of all copies, and the recording of a memory store as a processing activity, are the canvas's reading. In <code>V4_Evidence_Catalogue/EVIDENCE_PACK.md</code> §4.3, a Data Protection Impact Assessment applies at any tier where the processing is likely to result in a high risk to individuals (UK GDPR Art. 35(1)), in place of all tiers.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA Consumer Duty, <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.</strong> In the Operating Envelope Declaration example, the limb on vulnerable customers cites the Consumer Duty, which requires firms to take account of characteristics of vulnerability (PRIN 2A.2.25G). This replaces \"COBS 2.1R protections\". COBS 2.1.1R, the client's best interests rule, does not mention vulnerability. The Consumer Duty row of the Ten Materiality Dimensions table names the four outcomes of the Duty (PRIN 2A.3 to 2A.6) and the treatment of customers with characteristics of vulnerability. This replaces \"needs-based advice\", a term that PRIN 2A does not use.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) and FG22/5, IRQ-CON-010 and TP-CON-010.</strong> PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) provides that appropriate friction included to comply with PRIN 2A.6.2R(3) does not amount to an unreasonable barrier. It gives no figure. FG22/5 ¶9.20 says that what amounts to either \"will depend on the circumstances\", and expects firms to apply judgement. The text had said that the provision places the boundary in the assessor's judgement. The change is made in IRQ-CON-010 field 11, in the ★ statement (§1) and the materiality threshold (§5) of TP-CON-010, and in the disclosure requirement of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_CON.md</code>. The basis cell of the threshold adds FG22/5 ¶9.20. ★1 is unchanged, and the materiality of an asymmetry stays reviewer-set.</li>\n<li><strong>NCSC, \"Managing the cyber risk of agentic AI\".</strong> The quotation on default-deny network access opens with the qualifier of the source, \"Where possible\" (item 4 c). The change is made in IRQ-AGT-003 field 9 and in the row \"Managing the cyber risk of agentic AI\" in section 25 of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> (two places).</li>\n<li><strong>EU AI Act, read in the consolidated text of 27 July 2026 (CELEX 02024R1689-20260727).</strong></li>\n<li>Annex III insurance pricing is limited to life and health insurance, as Annex III point 5(c) states. The change is made in IRQ-DAT-001 field 17, IRQ-GOV-009 fields 9 and 17, IRQ-GOV-011 fields 2, 6 and 7, the headline finding of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_A.md</code>, and the section \"Notable 2025–2026 additions\" of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>.</li>\n<li>2 August 2026 is stated as the general date of application of the Act (Art. 113, second paragraph), and the Article 50 transparency obligations apply from that date. This replaces a date for Article 50 alone (IRQ-DAT-001 and IRQ-SEC-004 field 17).</li>\n<li>The headline finding of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_A.md</code> carries a dated note that says the same. The note adds that providers of systems placed on the market before that date must comply with Art. 50(2) by 2 December 2026 (Art. 111(4)).</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-009 field 17 gives both postponed dates: 2 December 2027 for Annex III systems and 2 August 2028 for Annex I systems (Art. 113, third paragraph, point (c), as amended). It adds the limb of Art. 111(2), as amended. A high-risk system placed on the market or put into service before that date comes under the Regulation only if its design changes significantly from that date. A system intended for use by public authorities must comply by 2 August 2030. This replaces a clause that left uses outside Annex III in scope.</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-001 and IRQ-SEC-004 field 17 state that providers of systems placed on the market before 2 August 2026 must comply with Art. 50(2) by 2 December 2026 (Art. 111(4), as amended). This replaces a date from which Art. 50(2) marking applies. The Date cell of the AI Act row in <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §5 says the same. In <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, the Art 50 worked example of the Dated Forward Obligation Trigger gives 2 August 2026 as the date from which the obligations apply (Art 113). For systems that generate synthetic content and were placed on the market before that date, it gives 2 December 2026 for Art 50(2) (Art 111(4)).</li>\n<li>The Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 row of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §5 adds Art 1(39)(a) of that Regulation, which replaces Art. 111(2). It states the limb that IRQ-AGT-009 field 17 states, with 2 August 2030 for high-risk systems intended for use by public authorities. The row names the amending act and the consolidated text as read on 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>IRQ-HUM-002 field 9 quotes Art. 14(4)(d) in its own words: the overseer must be enabled \"to decide, in any particular situation, not to use the high-risk AI system or to otherwise disregard, override or reverse the output of the high-risk AI system\". This replaces a shortened phrase in quotation marks, \"decide not to use\".</li>\n<li><strong>Field 17 verification notes.</strong> Where a field 17 line now rests on a source read on 22 October 2026 and carries a verification note, the note records that read (a line with no verification note does not gain one):</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-001: the AI Act text;</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-004: the AI Act text and the DSIT AI Cyber Security Code of Practice;</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-009: the AI Act text and the FCA AI Update;</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-013: the SS1/21 and SYSC 15A paragraphs;</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-008 and IRQ-DAT-012: SS1/23;</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-011: the DSIT AI Cyber Security Code of Practice;</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008: the SS1/21 and SYSC 15A paragraphs;</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-010: UK GDPR Articles 17 and 30;</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-009: the AI Act text;</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-003: the Bank of England and PRA letter and the FCA AI Update;</li>\n<li>43 field 17 lines that cite PRA SS1/23: the SS1/23 dates (section 1.4).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.3 Security frameworks cited for what they contain</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DSIT AI Cyber Security Code of Practice (January 2025) and its Implementation Guide.</strong> The canvases cite the Code for the provisions it contains. The Code has no data-flow mapping principle, no rule on secrets in AI system context, no provision on credential lifetime or revocation, and no principles for agentic systems.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-008 (fields 9, 11, 17, 18 and 21) and the confidence line of TP-SEC-008 cite provision 5.1, an inventory of assets that includes their interdependencies and connectivity. Fields 17 and 18 add provision 5.4, protection of sensitive data against unauthorised access. Fields 9 and 18 cite the example in the Implementation Guide of mapping threats and data flows under provision 3.1. Field 11 no longer names the Code or OWASP LLM02:2025 as the test method, because neither sets one out.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-006 field 17 cites provisions 6.1 and 5.4. It also cites the example that the Implementation Guide gives under provision 6.1: restrict access to system prompts and configuration data.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-011 (fields 9, 11, 17, 18 and 21), the confidence line of TP-SEC-011, IRQ-SEC-004 field 17 and IRQ-SEC-010 field 17 cite provision 2.6. Under that provision, permissions granted to an AI system on other systems are limited to what its function requires and are risk assessed. The Implementation Guide applies that provision to agentic systems. IRQ-SEC-011 field 9 rests the short-lived credential limb on general practice only. Field 11 cites the Code for the scope rule tested, not for the method.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Agentic AI Threats and Mitigations (Version 1.0, February 2025), LLM06:2025 and LLM10:2025.</strong> These sources describe risks and mitigations. None sets out a test method.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-003 field 11, and the ★ line and method classification of TP-AGT-003, cite LLM06:2025 and threat T2 Tool Misuse, in place of test guidance and a test pattern.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-005 field 11 and the method classification of TP-AGT-005 cite the threats and mitigations that the document describes, in place of test patterns.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-006 fields 9 and 11 and the method classification of TP-AGT-006 name the threat as the source names it, T4 Resource Overload, in place of a \"resource-exhaustion category\". They cite LLM10:2025 for mitigation guidance, in place of test guidance.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Machine Learning Security Top 10.</strong> None of its ten items names deserialisation. IRQ-TPR-005 (fields 11 and 21), the confidence line of TP-TPR-005 and the domain overview of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code> cite it for supply-chain risk only. Field 21 cites ML06:2023, whose model-hub scenario has malicious code from a downloaded model run in the organisation's environment. The ★3 of the component is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP LLM03:2025 Supply Chain and ML06:2023 ML Supply Chain Attacks.</strong> Neither entry's prevention and mitigation guidance names a serialisation format (LLM03:2025, Prevention and Mitigation Strategies; ML06:2023, How to Prevent). The serialisation-format control is now stated as the canvas's own.</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-005 field 9 and IRQ-SEC-009 field 9 cite LLM03:2025 for models from verifiable sources, with signing and file hashes to confirm model integrity, and say that the entry's prevention and mitigation strategies name no serialisation format. IRQ-TPR-005 field 9 had cited the entry for unsafe model serialisation formats.</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-005 field 18 says that the two entries document the model supply-chain and artefact-integrity risks, and that the prevention guidance of neither names serialisation formats. It had said that they document the unsafe-serialisation risk. Both entries were read again on 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-005 fields 11 and 14 state that limb (c) of the test, on serialisation formats, has no OWASP anchor. Field 14 anchors the ★3 component on the supply-chain risk of ML06:2023. The ★3 of the component and the ★2 of the canvas are unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_SEC.md</code>.</strong> The note on OWASP entry numbers says that the canvas files of the domain (the thirteen files CANVASES_SEC_001.md to CANVASES_SEC_014.md; IRQ-SEC-003 is in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>) cite the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications in its 2025 edition, with the <code>:2025</code> suffix or with the edition named in words. A 2026 entry number appears only as a read-across. The note had said that every citation in the file carries the suffix. The domain introduction says that direct injection/jailbreak and extraction testing generally rate ★3–★4 on published sources (OWASP, MITRE ATLAS). It also says that the basis of the ★4 of IRQ-SEC-001 is under review as URO-8. It had called these sources published methodology. It had also named NIST AI 100-2, which no canvas for those tests cites.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-002 field 10.</strong> The L4 evidence is an independent red-team report whose technique coverage draws on published OWASP and MITRE ATLAS sources. It had said that the coverage is scoped per OWASP/MITRE methodology. ★3 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, Tier 2.</strong> The bullet on benchmarked test methods names a published methodology, such as an OWASP testing guide or a NIST SP 800-53 control assessment, and refers to <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3. This replaces \"(OWASP, NIST)\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.4 Dates and editions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ARP 4761.</strong> SAE International issued ARP 4761 in December 1996 and revised it as ARP4761A in December 2023 (SAE Mobilus, the ARP4761 and ARP4761A pages). IRQ-GOV-012 field 20 entry [5] is dated 1996, in place of 2010, a year that no edition carries, and names the revision. The ARP 4761 entry under References &amp; Standards in <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> gives both dates.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Agentic Security Initiative threat model.</strong> Its cover gives Version 1.0, February 2025. The ★2 rationale of IRQ-SEC-004 field 11 and the pitfalls section of TP-SEC-004 give that date. They had said that the threat model was \"under a year old\", which the canvas's own dates contradict.</li>\n<li><strong>PRA SS1/23, the dates of the April 2026 version.</strong> The cover of the April 2026 version gives \"16 April 2026 (updating 17 May 2023)\". The PRA's page for the version gives \"Published 23 April 2026. Effective from 23 April 2026.\" The text of 16 April 2026 keeps para 1.5, under which the policy came into effect on 17 May 2024. Each citation of the April 2026 version keeps its cover date and adds that the version was published and took effect on 23 April 2026.</li>\n<li>The limb \"published and effective 23 April 2026\" is added at 140 sites in fields 9, 17 and 20 of 69 canvases: IRQ-AGT-001 to IRQ-AGT-013; IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-DAT-003 to IRQ-DAT-008 and IRQ-DAT-011 to IRQ-DAT-014; IRQ-GOV-001 to IRQ-GOV-012; IRQ-MOD-001 to IRQ-MOD-005 and IRQ-MOD-007 to IRQ-MOD-016; IRQ-RES-005 and IRQ-RES-006; IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002, IRQ-SEC-004 to IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-SEC-013 and IRQ-SEC-014; IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-004 and IRQ-TPR-005; and the exemplar canvases for IRQ-SEC-003 and IRQ-MOD-006.</li>\n<li>At 51 of these sites, in field 17, field 20 or both, the citation also gives the policy as in effect since 17 May 2024 (para 1.5). This replaces a single effective date of May 2024 or 17 May 2024. These sites are in 32 canvases: IRQ-AGT-001; IRQ-DAT-001 and IRQ-DAT-014; IRQ-GOV-001 to IRQ-GOV-012; IRQ-MOD-001 to IRQ-MOD-005 and IRQ-MOD-007 to IRQ-MOD-016; and the exemplar canvases for IRQ-SEC-003 and IRQ-MOD-006.</li>\n<li>Where a field 17 line that gains the limb \"published and effective 23 April 2026\" carries a verification note, the note records that the SS1/23 dates were read on 22 October 2026 (43 lines). The notes of IRQ-DAT-008 and IRQ-DAT-012 already record a read of SS1/23 on that date. A changed line with no verification note does not gain one.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008 field 20 no longer repeats the publication date after \"amended by LIAF01/26\".</li>\n<li><code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.3 gives the April 2026 version as \"published and effective 23 April 2026\", in place of \"amendment published 23 April 2026\".</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-014 keeps \"effective 17 May 2024\" for the May 2023 version, which the PRA's page gives as effective from that date. Its citations of the April 2026 version already gave the limb.</li>\n<li><strong>PoisonedRAG (Zou et al.).</strong> The paper is an arXiv preprint of 2024 (arXiv:2402.07867), published in the 34th USENIX Security Symposium (USENIX Security 25), 2025, pp. 3827–3844 (the USENIX record of the paper). IRQ-SEC-005 fields 18 and 20, and the PoisonedRAG row of <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>, add the USENIX publication. Fields 18 and 20 of the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003 give the year 2024 and the arXiv number, with the 2025 USENIX publication, in place of the year 2025. Field 21 of IRQ-SEC-005 calls the research peer-reviewed, and the USENIX publication is the source of that status.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.5 Addresses</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-008 field 20, entry [4].</strong> The entry gives the FCA Handbook addresses of DISP and PRIN 2A, in place of the Handbook home page.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-001 field 20, entry [5].</strong> The entry states that its original address redirected to an unrelated page when read on 22 October 2026, and points to the archived copy of the release.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>2. Statements about the standard</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code>, Cross-Domain Links.</strong> The two examples name links that the canvases carry in field 19 and that <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> records. IRQ-MOD-013 links to IRQ-RES-004 (G.2, Containment &amp; Recovery), because a rollback of prompts and configuration is part of a full rollback of behavioural components. This replaces IRQ-HUM-007. IRQ-DAT-007 links to IRQ-SEC-003, in place of IRQ-SEC-004.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-014 field 1.</strong> The applicability note gives the Y for generative systems as the reason why the question is not restricted to agentic deployments. It had given the N on the third limb, which is the predictive limb.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-HUM-009.</strong> The confidence line marks two of the six expected controls as Emerging Practice, in place of two of five. The canvas added the EU AI Act Article 86 control at v1.3.3.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-RES-001 §6.</strong> The Partial interpretation no longer says that the canvas names it the most common outcome. The canvas ranks no outcome as the most common.</li>\n<li><strong>Domain overviews of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_HUM.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code> and <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code>.</strong> The overviews no longer state that assurance objectives and pass, fail and partial indicators are carried across unchanged. ★ ratings are carried unchanged.</li>\n<li>Each of the three overviews states that assurance objectives and pass and fail indicators are restated from the canvases. Some objectives and some indicators keep the canvas's words, and the rest are reworded. Where a procedure and its canvas differ, the canvas governs (<code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2). The overviews no longer name partial indicators, in line with §2 of the overview as this release states it.</li>\n<li>In <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code>, a result maps to the canvas's pass and fail indicators and its reporting language, in place of pass/fail/partial indicators. Field 11 is described as naming a published methodology where one exists, because TP-SEC-012 runs no technical test.</li>\n<li><strong>Technical limbs in staging, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code>.</strong> The domain overview and operating rule 1 name TP-TPR-005 with TP-TPR-004 and TP-TPR-006 as procedures with a technical limb run in isolated staging. TP-TPR-005's limb is its bounded behavioural probe.</li>\n<li><strong>Reviewer-days.</strong> In the time/effort estimate of TP-SEC-002 (<code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_002.md</code>), the estimate is 5.5 reviewer-days, the sum of its stated parts (0.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 1.0), in place of 5.0. <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code> gives the Security domain total as 57.5 reviewer-days, in place of 57.0, in its total indicative effort and in its effort table. <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §9 gives the programme total as 455.5 reviewer-days, in place of 455.0, and its calibration note follows. No ★ rating or test step changes.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_DAT.md</code>, shared test-environment setup.</strong> The environment supports twelve of the original thirteen procedures, in place of eleven, as the component table lists them (all but TP-DAT-003).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-010 field 20, entry [4].</strong> The entry says that the paper of Zac et al. was read at the primary on 3 October 2026, as field 18 records. It had said that the paper was not read at the primary.</li>\n<li><strong>Method classes, <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3.</strong> Two entries are added. \"None established\" means that no published testing methodology exists for the question; <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.4 keeps this method class distinct from a control marked \"Emerging Practice\". \"Two classes\" lets a label name two of the classes as \"A / B\", where the parts of a test fall in different classes, and the procedure states which part falls in which class. The standing rule on honesty of method in §1 of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> names \"None established\" and states the two-class rule.</li>\n<li>TP-AGT-003: the method classification reads Emerging Practice, in place of Adapted Industry Practice. Its sources, OWASP LLM06:2025 mitigation guidance and threat T2, set out no test method (section 1.3). ★2 is unchanged.</li>\n<li>TP-AGT-006 and TP-TPR-005: the method class reads \"Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice\", in place of \"Mixed\". The TP-TPR-005 row of the domain table in <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code> reads the same. The text of each procedure states its two parts. ★2 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Grading, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2.</strong> The pass and fail positions originate in canvas fields 12 and 13. Field 14 gives the reporting language for each result and is not a grade rule. The procedure's own grade rule decides whether a result is Partial. Where the facts meet a fail indicator and a partial indicator, the result is Fail. This replaces the statement that all three positions originate in fields 12, 13 and 14.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §1, In scope.</strong> The statement adds that, for a ★1 or ★2 procedure, two reviewers may still design the test differently or reach different conclusions (§8).</li>\n<li><strong>Compiled lines.</strong> The heads of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> and of the AGT, CON, DAT, MOD and RES domain playbooks point to the change log for later changes.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_DAT.md</code>, domain-level evidence maturity caveat.</strong> Input drift (TP-DAT-011) is placed at ★3, and retrieval and embedding drift (TP-DAT-012) at ★2, as the canvases rate them and as the effort summary lists them. The caveat had placed all drift testing at ★3. No ★ rating moves.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-RES-001 §3.</strong> The canvas is said to identify the untrusted-output trigger as a weak point whose trigger is often left undefined. It had been called near-universal.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-AGT-012.</strong> The ★ statement, the method classification and the canvas-limitation pitfall refer to two published worked examples of layered gate design, as the canvas does. These are the OpenAI Operator system card (23 January 2025) and Meta's Muse agent security disclosure (8 September 2026). They had referred to one. ★2 and the method class are unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-012 fields 11, 14 and 21.</strong> The test adds evidence limb (6): the provider's release notes and the firm's version-pinning records for the period, reviewed for any capability that the model or platform gained without a change made by the firm. Evidence limb (2) also names the customer segments actually served, checked against the declared segment, as step 4 of TP-GOV-012 does. Each vector of the six-vector taxonomy that the ★2 rationale names now has an evidence limb, as step 7 of TP-GOV-012 already has. The pass statement of field 14 names the customer segment and silent capability among the tested vectors. The evidence list of Test 3 in the field 21 footer names segment records, the provider's release notes and the firm's version-pinning records. Each list now has one item for each of the six evidence limbs of field 11. ★2 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003, field 10.</strong> The red-team payload classes are the five that field 11 names: instruction override, goal hijack, tool-invocation, data-exfiltration, and delayed or conditional triggers. Persona-switch, which field 11 does not name, is removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003, field 14.</strong> The pass statement says that testing draws on OWASP LLM01 (2025) guidance, under review as URO-7. It had said that testing follows the LLM01 methodology, which URO-7 records that LLM01:2025 does not give. ★4 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-001 field 14.</strong> The pass statement says that testing draws on OWASP LLM01 (2025) guidance, under review as URO-8, and on MITRE ATLAS AML.T0051 case studies. It had said that testing follows the LLM01 and MITRE ATLAS methodology. URO-8 records that LLM01:2025 gives no test method. Field 11 names the MITRE ATLAS source as AML.T0051 case studies. ★4 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-008 to IRQ-GOV-012, field 17.</strong> The [UNVERIFIED] tag on section pinpoints lists only the instruments that each canvas cites. Each new list is a part of the old list, and no instrument is added.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code>, examples of correct allocation.</strong> The examples for IRQ-MOD-015 and IRQ-HUM-004 quote each question in full, as <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> gives it, in place of a shortened form. The IRQ-MOD-015 example adds the four limbs of the question, from \"known bias classes\" to \"correlated error assessed\". The IRQ-HUM-004 example adds \"with oversight capacity dimensioned against AI throughput and reconstructable behaviour\".</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, Governance.</strong> A sentence says that the headings number the four layers Layer 2, Layer 3, Layer 4 and Final Layer, and that there is no Layer 1.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, Phase 5, Gate 5 (Opinion Drafted &amp; Peer-Reviewed).</strong> The first criterion requires that the opinion is signed and that the signer's independence is confirmed. It no longer refers to an authorised signer. <code>00_Admin/OWNERSHIP_AND_LICENCE.md</code> states that no person or body is authorised to certify, accredit or approve anything against FAIAS.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>Part H.1 is the Regulatory Alignment Summary, in place of the Compliance Status Summary. Its status column reads Aligned, Conditionally aligned or Not aligned, in place of the compliance terms. Its interpretation states that the table records the reviewer's view of alignment and is not a determination of compliance.</li>\n<li>Part C states that the ★ values in its table are illustrative and are not the register's. At this version the two tests rated ★4, IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003, support CLM-09 only, so no other claim can show ★4.</li>\n<li>The heading of Part J.4 labels its counts as those of the illustrative engagement of Part J.3, not the register's.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code>.</strong> In Component 6, Part B, the sub-claims are a separate, illustrative argument, not the core claims of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code>, and Part C of the opinion reports against the register's claims. The Part H outline of Component 7 uses the alignment terms of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> Part H.1.</li>\n<li><strong><code>README.md</code>, the bullet on the correction pass of 29 July 2026.</strong> The pointer names <code>CANVASES_CON_003.md</code>, <code>CANVASES_CON_005.md</code>, <code>CANVASES_CON_006.md</code> and <code>CANVASES_CON_008.md</code>, whose Independent Test states each rating, in place of the domain file <code>CANVASES_CON.md</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-010, change log.</strong> A note says that the v1.2.0 entry is dated 2026-09-14, the date of its erratum. Every other v1.2.0 entry in the standard is dated 2026-09-12, and v1.2.0 was signed on 15 September 2026. The dated entry stays as written.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.1 (Version-anchored ratings).</strong> The rule states that the ★ rating in each canvas carries its anchor inside the rating artefact, in place of every ★ rating. Field 11 of each of the 109 canvases carries the anchor, \"as at FAIAS\" with the version and its date. The ★ lines of the test procedures carry the rating from the canvas, with no anchor. No ★ rating moves.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. Source registers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ISO/IEC 27090 (<code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>; <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>).</strong> The standard is published. The Date cell of the register row reads \"International Standard published (stage 60.60), Edition 1, publication date 2026-10 (read by the maintainer 22 Oct 2026)\", and the Type cell reads \"Standard\". The Relevance cell makes the row a crosswalk source for FAIAS Security, cited at the level of the standard, with no clause mapped, in place of a watchlist entry. Its verification note adds the read of 22 October 2026 at the ISO catalogue record: stage 60.60, Edition 1 and publication date 2026-10. The record of the reads of 29 July and 7 October 2026 stays. In §4 (Maintenance model) of <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>, the \"Known-aging content\" bullet no longer names ISO/IEC 27090 among in-flight standards.</li>\n<li><strong>CFPB, \"Chatbots in consumer finance\" (6 June 2023), <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §16.</strong> An entry is added after the entry for the CFPB circular. It describes the report as the CFPB's own research, not a rule or guidance. It records complaints about \"doom loops\" with no \"offramp to a human customer service representative\". The report states that the limits of these chatbots \"may not be apparent to customers when they are initially signing up\". It also states that their deployment \"may also be an intentional choice by entities seeking to grow revenue or minimize write offs\". The entry makes the report a US supervisory signal for IRQ-CON-010. The report names firms as examples of chatbot adoption and technology, names no firm as the subject of the complaints or of these practices, and measures no asymmetry. The verification note records that the whole report was read at the Internet Archive's capture of the publisher's PDF. The additions line at the head of the register and the Summary record the addition: 163 entries + 1 = 164. The regulatory register row of <code>README.md</code> gives 164 register entries, in place of 163.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code>.</strong> The row \"Expanding Daybreak as the Cyber Defense Window Narrows\" gives the completion rates as the page reports them, on advanced cybersecurity requests that include exploit-chain development and authentication bypass. The rates are 95.0% for GPT-5.6-Cyber, available through Daybreak Red, 1.5% for GPT-5.6 Sol, and 2.0% for GPT-5.6 Sol with Daybreak Blue access. This replaces a 95.0% against 1.5% differential tied to Daybreak Blue. The \"Path to Astra\" row quotes the safeguards sentence whole, from \"Based on retrospective testing\". It no longer says that the control was not tested against the event.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong> The MEMOREPAIR row counts the paper as one of the three independent 2026 author groups that IRQ-RES-004 field 18 counts, with ChronoMem and Yu et al. It says that field 18 cites the paper for the cascade mechanism, in place of \"a fourth author group\" and a cascade mechanism asserted without a citation. The row of Mohan et al. (ARIES) says that the paper anchors the generic limb of IRQ-RES-004, which v1.2.0 re-anchored from \"established practice\".</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>, IRQ-MOD-016.</strong> The evidence cell attributes the evaluation-awareness rates of 41.1% (xhigh) and 50.6% (max reasoning effort) to Apollo Research's measurement, as OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra System Card reports it (§8.8.1). It had said that the publishers measured them.</li>\n<li><strong>NIST control overlays (<code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §10; <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>).</strong> On 20 October 2026 NIST renamed the project SP 800-53 Control Overlays for Super Intelligence Systems (COSIS). Documents issued before the rename were not altered and keep the old name (the NIST project page, read 22 October 2026). The register row gives the new title with the former name, Control Overlays for Securing AI Systems (COSAiS), and the new address. Its Date cell names the draft of 8 January 2026 as the latest draft, and adds the rename. A dated note after the note of 7 October 2026 records the rename. The \"Known-aging content\" bullet of <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code> §4 names NIST COSIS, named COSAiS until 20 October 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>Other rows of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>Head: a dated note says that a row that records a verification gives it and its date in the row. A row with no recorded verification and no [UNVERIFIED] mark records none. The absence of the mark does not show that the row was verified.</li>\n<li>§4, the row for the White Paper and the government response: the address cell gives the addresses of CP 815 and of CP 1019, the government response of 6 February 2024. It had given the White Paper address only.</li>\n<li>§4, the row for the ICO Code and the ADM guidance: a currency note of 22 October 2026 says that the ICO plans page no longer lists the update of the Automated Decision Making (ADM) and Profiling Guidance. An Internet Archive capture of the page of 4 October 2026 still lists it, with \"Winter 2026\". The row now carries no target date for that guidance, and the note of 7 October 2026 stays as the record of its read.</li>\n<li>§12, the row for OWASP Agentic AI: Threats and Mitigations: the currency note names the two threats that the standard cites by number, T2 Tool Misuse and T4 Resource Overload. Each carries the same number and name in v1.0 and v1.1, so the citations are unaffected. The note had said that no entry was cited by number.</li>\n<li>§13, the MITRE ATLAS v2026.08 row: a currency note of 22 October 2026 says that release v2026.09, published 15 September 2026, supersedes the pin, and gives its counts from its release notes. The pin does not move. The one technique identifier that the standard cites, AML.T0051, rests on the v2026.08 data, and the ATLAS counts cited at IRQ-SEC-013 carry their own release date.</li>\n<li>§27, the Bank of Japan FY2026 survey row: the row quotes the summary finding, \"Over 90 percent of financial institutions are using or trialing GenAI\". It then quotes in full the sentence of the annex body that gives the base of that figure. About 80 percent of the institutions use GenAI, and over 90 percent when those under trial are included. The row had quoted only the end of that sentence. The verification note records the read of 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>§28, the CWA 18398:2026 row: the first quoted sentence is labelled the summary statement and the second the mission, as the role profile at Annex B §5.4 labels them. The row had labelled both sentences the mission. The verification note records the read of 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>Summary: the figure 105 is the total without the ten watch items entered on 25 August 2026. The three watchlist entries already within the 87 stay counted.</li>\n<li>Summary: the paragraph on the addition of 22 October 2026 names the ten existing entries that were rewritten and are not counted as additions. It had said that no existing entry was rewritten on that date.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>The Wu and Xiao row links to version 1 of the paper, which the row cites and quotes. A note says that version 2 of 21 October 2026 gives the paper a new title (the arXiv record, read 22 October 2026).</li>\n<li>The Xu row quotes the paper's own words, \"one trace cannot decide them\" (arXiv 2609.07162v1). It had quoted \"a single trace cannot decide this\".</li>\n<li>The AI Risk Repository row adds a currency note. The version of August 2024 lists 777 risks from 43 taxonomies. The current version, v3 of 5 May 2026, analyses 74 frameworks with 1,725 distinct risks and is published in Patterns (Cell Press, 2026) (the arXiv record, read 22 October 2026).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>The row \"Improving our alignment and security efforts\" states that an independent review with METR is planned, as the page states it (\"We are also planning to work with METR for an independent review\"). It had said that the review was commissioned. The entry of 9 September 2026 records the signed agreement.</li>\n<li>The Date cell of the row \"Introducing Claude Fable 5.1 and Claude Mythos 5.1\" says that the page prints the month and year only. It had said that the page prints no date.</li>\n<li><strong>Heads of the registers.</strong> The Compiled line of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code> and <code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code> says that the register is revised in later releases.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Incident register</h2>\n<h3>4.1 Rows 48, 61, 71 and 79: the primary marker</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code>.</strong> Rows 48, 61, 71 and 79 carry [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] in place of the secondary form. This applies the decision of 8 October 2026, which the text signed at v1.3.3 did not carry. Each row rests in part on a party's own document or statement. These are the affected user's own posts on X (row 48), the company's own Form 10-Q (row 61), the plaintiffs' own pleading (row 71) and the vendor's own CVE record (row 79). Those documents were read again on 22 October 2026. The source type that the secondary form named stands in each Source cell before the marker. The note of 9 October 2026 at the foot of the register now counts rows 47 to 91 as 30 rows with [primary verified 7 Oct 2026], these four rows, and 11 rows with the secondary form. A dated note at the foot records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Canvases.</strong> IRQ-AGT-002 (row 48), IRQ-DAT-011 (row 61), IRQ-HUM-002 (row 71), IRQ-SEC-014 (row 79) and the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003 (row 79) no longer call these rows secondary rows in field 18. Each citation keeps the limit it states: the third-party transcript for the machine-learning detail of row 61, the pleading of row 71, and the independent case study for the mechanism of row 79. The v1.3.3 change-log lines of these canvases stay as records. No ★ rating, claim criticality, severity value or deliberate gap changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.2 Rows read again on 22 October 2026</h3>\n<p>The legend now names the notes of 9 and 22 October 2026 at the foot of the register. A note of 22 October 2026 at the foot lists each result below. Rows 4 and 22 were searched again, no primary was located, and they keep their marker. Row 17 keeps its marker.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary marker on the issuing party's own document.</strong></li>\n<li>Row 1: the failure mode follows the decision in Moffatt v. Air Canada, 2024 BCCRT 149 (Civil Resolution Tribunal, 14 February 2024, paras 14 to 17 and 27). The decision records that Air Canada gave no information about the nature of its chatbot. The words \"separate legal entity\" are the tribunal's reading of the airline's defence.</li>\n<li>Row 15: SEC press release 2024-36 (18 March 2024). The charges are settled charges of false and misleading statements about the use of AI, made in SEC filings, a press release, websites and social media. \"AI washing\" is the label that the SEC's Chair and its Director of Enforcement used. The firms neither admitted nor denied the findings.</li>\n<li>Row 27: Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme, Report, Volume 1 (7 July 2023, pp. v and x). The Federal Court declaration of 2019 was made with the Government's consent.</li>\n<li>Row 29: National Audit Office, HC 2144 (24 May 2019). The classification was ETS's voice-recognition software, with each result reviewed by two ETS human listeners and a further ETS review of the patterns at each test centre (Summary para 2). At least 11,356 people associated with an invalid or questionable result had left the UK by March 2019, some 2,468 of them by enforced removal (para 3.27). The row gives the two caveats of the NAO: people may have left for other reasons, and the numbers may be underestimates.</li>\n<li>Row 32: one sentence from the same source is added for balance (the risk report, p.148).</li>\n<li>Row 37: the gains from stolen AI credentials are attributed to the general statement of the report (p.30), not to the actor of the row.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary marker for the user's own post on X.</strong> The decision of 22 October 2026 applies to rows 3 and 10 the decision of 8 October 2026. Row 3 adds the customer's instruction to the bot to \"disregard any rules\", from the customer's own screenshots. Row 10 adds the user's instruction to the bot, from the user's own screenshots. The firm's side of each row rests on reporting, which its Source cell names. The note of 19 September 2026 stays true of the report it describes.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary marker for first-party limbs.</strong></li>\n<li>Row 11: the harm is given in NEDA's own words, from NEDA's own post of 30 May 2023, which the note of 26 September 2026 recorded as unread. The dieting content and the vendor's role are attributed to the press.</li>\n<li>Row 16: the Hong Kong Government's written reply in the Legislative Council (LCQ9, 26 June 2024, reply part (1)) is the source. It records a pre-recorded video conference and payment instructions given over instant messaging. It records a loss of about HK$200 million to five local bank accounts. The row also cites the firm's own report (Arup, Annual and ESG Report FY2023/24, p.7). The 15 transfers and the firm's identity are attributed to the press.</li>\n<li>Row 21: the demonstration is dated to Google's own post of 6 February 2023, and NASA's page dates the first image of an exoplanet to 2004. The fall in market value is attributed to the press.</li>\n<li>Row 28: the row gives the figures of the agency's own review (Michigan Talent Investment Agency, 11 August 2017). Of 62,784 unappealed fraud cases (October 2013 to August 2015), 40,195 were resolved by the computer program, and 85% of those findings were later reversed. 22,589 were referred to an investigator, and 44% of those were reversed. The agency says that most reversals were overpayments without intent to defraud. \"No human review\" is limited to the cases that the program resolved. The 93% figure stays as pleaded only. The pleadings attribute it to the state's audit office, whose MiDAS audit states that it did not review the identification of fraudulent claims (report 641-0593-15, February 2016, p.30). The 85% figure that the correction log of 19 September 2026 removed as unsourced is in the agency's review.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary form, with the source type after the dash.</strong></li>\n<li>Row 19: the word \"RAG\" is removed from the failure mode and the domain cell, because no source read states the chatbot's architecture. The row cites The Markup (29 March 2024) and the city's release of 16 October 2023.</li>\n<li>Row 26: the dataset is described as holding links, not images, as LAION states (19 December 2023). The finding of the Stanford Internet Observatory (19 December 2023) is read at its archived copy.</li>\n<li>Row 33: the CVSS version is named, v4.0 9.4, and the CVE record also carries v3.1 9.6.</li>\n<li>Row 34: the statement that the chain is not prompt injection is marked as the register's own reading. The source does not use the term.</li>\n<li>Row 35: the figures for the Cursor-driven subset are attributed to Gambit Security (27 August 2026), whose report is their origin. The note of 12 September 2026 stays as the dated record.</li>\n<li>Row 36: one sentence from the same source is added for balance (the BIS FSI paper, Box A).</li>\n<li><strong>Rows 47 to 91 read again.</strong> Rows 50 and 80 add a later document of the vendor that does not refer to the disclosure (GitHub changelog, 10 December 2025; Salesforce release note on Trusted URL enforcement, from 8 September 2025). Both keep the secondary form. Row 64 is now read in full, at the published version in eScholarship, and keeps the secondary form. Row 71 adds the defendant's filed answer (Doc. 57, filed 2 May 2025, ECF pp.2, 3 and 6). Row 79 gives Microsoft's own record (Security Update Guide, CVE-2025-32711, revision 1.2, 20 February 2026) as the source of its fix and exploitation limbs. Row 86 adds the vendor's hardening guide, read at an Internet Archive capture of 27 June 2024. Row 88 adds the scanner changes of picklescan pull requests #33 and #35. The note of 9 October 2026 gives the new source types of rows 64 and 86. Its counts for rows 47 to 91 stay true.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.3 Rows 92 to 101 added</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Row 92</strong>, IBM Financial Transaction Manager, CVE-2026-18875: an unauthenticated write path into the runbook store of an AI agent server in payments software (IBM security bulletin, first published 21 September 2026). It is a vendor's record of a defect in shipped software, entered on the inclusion note of 12 September 2026. It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-SEC-005.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 93</strong>, a campaign against South Korean financial organisations with an agentic penetration-testing tool (CrowdStrike, 7 October 2026; Yonhap, 1 October 2026; the FSC and FSS consumer alert of 6 October 2026). It carries the secondary form and states the attribution grade in words. It carries no personal detail of the suspected actor, and no canvas cites it.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 94</strong>, Railway: an agent deleted a production volume with an account-scoped API token that it found on the developer's machine (Railway, 29 April 2026; changelog of 1 May 2026). It rests on the platform provider's own account, which names neither the customer nor the agent. It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-AGT-003 and IRQ-SEC-011.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 95</strong>, Open WebUI, CVE-2026-45398: retrieval endpoints that did not apply the access control for knowledge-base collections (project advisory GHSA-4g37-7p2c-38r9, 10 May 2026). It rests on the project's own advisory, carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026], and supports IRQ-DAT-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 96</strong>, the ACCEPT deskilling study: the adenoma detection rate of colonoscopy done without AI fell after four centres introduced AI (Budzyń et al., Lancet Gastroenterology &amp; Hepatology 10(10):896–903, October 2025). It is labelled an observational study of a deployed practice, not a harm event. It carries the secondary form for a peer-reviewed study read at its abstract, and supports IRQ-HUM-003.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 97</strong>, Oversight Board case 2020-004-IG-UA (28 January 2021): an automated classifier removed a post, and the company had stated that not all appeals receive human review. The row rests on the Oversight Board's own decision, which is not a court or a regulator. It carries the secondary form, with the source type after the dash: an oversight body's own decision, with the company's statements as the Board records them. It supports IRQ-HUM-008.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 98</strong>, Claude 3.7 Sonnet special-casing of tests, from the developer's system card (sections 6 and 6.1, p.22). It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-MOD-007 and IRQ-MOD-008.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 99</strong>, OpenAI o3: reward hacking of a scoring function and false reports by the model about its actions, from the developer's system card (16 April 2025, sections 3.9.1 and 3.9.2). The outside evaluators tested the models before release, with early checkpoints and the final launch candidates (section 3.9), and METR's findings concern earlier checkpoints. The fabricated-action limb rests on an outside evaluator's report (Transluce, 16 April 2025), which the Source cell names. It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-008 and IRQ-MOD-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 100</strong>, the LiteLLM 1.107.0 supply-chain compromise of 21 October 2026. A poisoned release of an open-source AI gateway was signed and published through the developer's own release pipeline (LiteLLM, account last updated 21 October 2026, 20:30 UTC; OSV MAL-2026-2144; PyPI). A second limb gives three LiteLLM entries in CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue (CVE-2026-42208, CVE-2026-42271 and CVE-2026-59822; catalogue version 2026.10.21). The stealer's database query and the later use of stolen credentials rest on a security firm's report (Wiz, 21 October 2026), which the Source cell names. The root cause is not confirmed, and the row gives each of the developer's statements at the strength that the developer gives it. The row is entered on the inclusion note of 12 September 2026, carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026], and supports IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-011.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 101</strong>, Libra Internet Bank: the bank's customer-service assistant, Lia, called a customer „idiotule” (idiot) in a chat, and the bank apologised and added prohibitions. The exchange rests on a press report of the image of the chat that the customer published (Wall-Street.ro, 18 September 2026). The bank's account of the cause, its apology and its added prohibitions rest on the bank's own statement on its LinkedIn company page, headed „Lia (prin OpenAI)”, which does not quote the reply. The sources do not date the exchange. The row quotes both sources in Romanian, each quotation with the standard's own translation. It carries the secondary form, with the source of each limb after the dash. It is in financial services and supports IRQ-CON-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Inclusion note of 22 October 2026.</strong> A note at the head of the register admits a peer-reviewed observational study of a deployed practice, with no harm event. The condition is that the study measures the quantity that an Independent Review Question asks about. Such a row is labelled in its Incident cell. Row 96 is the first.</li>\n<li><strong>Notes at the foot.</strong> The notes of 22 October 2026 give the source and the marker of each new row. Rows 94 to 100 are outside financial services, as their Institution cells say. Row 101 is in financial services, and its note states why it carries the secondary form. No IRQ star rating, claim criticality, severity value or deliberate gap changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.4 Rows linked to further questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ(s) supported.</strong> The cells of rows 2, 17, 47, 56, 63, 66, 69 and 70 name further questions whose canvases now cite the rows: row 2, IRQ-GOV-008; row 17, IRQ-AGT-003; row 47, IRQ-AGT-010; row 56, IRQ-GOV-011; row 63, IRQ-MOD-016 and IRQ-RES-004; row 66, IRQ-GOV-004 and IRQ-TPR-003; row 69, IRQ-HUM-007; row 70, IRQ-HUM-008.</li>\n<li><strong>Text added at the cited sources.</strong> Row 2 adds the 97 automated e-mails that were not acted upon before the market opened (SEC order, Release No. 34-70694, para 19). Row 17 adds the customer's later account of the agent's access to production databases (SaaStr, 2 August 2025). Row 47 adds, from Anthropic's own Project Vend report, the hallucinated payment account and the restocking contact who did not exist. Row 56 adds the Attorney General's allegations at paras 57 and 60, and its pinpoints follow. Row 63 adds, from the developer's own account, the rollback that took around 24 hours. Row 66 adds paras 2.59, 2.65 and 2.66 of the ANAO audit report (pp.37 to 39), and its pinpoints follow. Row 69 adds para 66 of the Wisconsin Supreme Court's decision in State v. Loomis. Each of these rows keeps its marker. The text of row 70 does not change.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.5 <code>README.md</code></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The incident register row gives 101 publicly documented incidents, in place of 91. It labels row 96 as one observational study of a deployed practice with no harm event, beside the four automation or non-AI analogues and the one audit finding.</li>\n<li><strong>The version line, the banner and the errata record.</strong> The version line names this release, 1.3.4, in place of 1.3.3, and the banner gives its date, 22 October 2026, in place of 9 October 2026. The errata record names this correction log, <code>00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-22.md</code>, as \"v1.3.4, changes to text signed at v1.3.3\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. Canvas citations of incident rows, and other research-basis additions</h2>\n<p>Each citation of a register row below is in the reserved form. Each canvas's change log records its change. No ★ rating or risk-profile value changes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Citations restated after the new reading.</strong></li>\n<li>IRQ-HUM-009 field 18: the MiDAS limb (row 28) gives the figures of the agency's review and keeps the 93% as pleaded only. It records that the state's audit did not review the identification of fraudulent claims. The TOEIC limb (row 29) describes the classifications as software with review by the testing firm's own listeners, and gives the departures and caveats of the NAO.</li>\n<li>IRQ-CON-004 field 18: the NYC MyCity citation no longer says \"RAG grounding\". It says that the city presented the chatbot as drawing on its own business web pages. The two incidents are called chatbot mis-statements, not generative ones, because the decision in Moffatt records that Air Canada gave no information about the nature of its chatbot, and no source read for row 19 states the chatbot's architecture. The field cites the two incidents in the reserved form, as register rows 1 and 19, with the limits of each row. Row 1 is a small-claims decision of a tribunal, in aviation. Row 19 rests on a newsroom's own testing and records no ruling, so the field no longer says that both incidents crystallised as liability.</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-014 field 18 (row 64): the study is read in full, and the row stays secondary.</li>\n<li>IRQ-HUM-002 field 18 (row 71): the defendant's filed answer is added. It denies the paragraph 3 allegations and that PxDx is an algorithm.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-014 field 18 (row 86), IRQ-AGT-004 field 18 (row 50) and IRQ-SEC-005 field 18 (row 80): each records the vendor's later document in place of \"no statement from the vendor was read\". Each row stays secondary.</li>\n<li><strong>Citations of rows 92 to 101.</strong> IRQ-SEC-005 (row 92). IRQ-AGT-003 and IRQ-SEC-011 (row 94). IRQ-DAT-009 (row 95). IRQ-HUM-003 (row 96). IRQ-HUM-008 (row 97). IRQ-MOD-007 and IRQ-MOD-008 (rows 98 and 99). IRQ-MOD-009 (row 99). IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-011 (row 100). IRQ-CON-009 (row 101).</li>\n<li>IRQ-CON-009 field 18 cites row 101 as a financial-services instance of harmful output to a customer in a service exchange, carried as a secondary row. The press report gives only the last turns, and the bank's statement refers to the language that the assistant was exposed to. So the row does not show that the use was wholly non-adversarial, and the field's [UNVERIFIED] record of no such financial-services incident stands.</li>\n<li><strong>Citations of rows already in the register.</strong> IRQ-AGT-003 (row 17). IRQ-AGT-010 (row 47). IRQ-GOV-004 and IRQ-TPR-003 (row 66, an audit finding of control gaps, not a harm event). IRQ-GOV-008 (row 2, a pre-AI automation analogue); its analogue sentence now says that one such finding is cited. IRQ-GOV-011 (row 56, allegations that the firm denies). IRQ-HUM-007 (row 69). IRQ-HUM-008 (row 70). IRQ-RES-004 field 18 (row 63). IRQ-MOD-016 cites row 63 in field 10, as a worked example of expected evidence; its research-basis date does not move.</li>\n<li><strong>Research basis without a register row.</strong> These two citations are not in the reserved form.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-006 field 18 cites Anthropic, \"How we built our multi-agent research system\" (13 June 2025). Its early agents spawned 50 subagents for simple queries, and its limit was written as scaling rules in the prompts. The canvas reads the post as a record of behaviour found and fixed before release, on the way from prototype to production, so it cites the post and not a register row.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-013 field 18 cites Anthropic, \"Developing a computer use model\" (22 October 2024). During demonstrations for the public beta, the model clicked to stop a screen recording, and the footage was lost. Its view of the screen can miss short-lived actions or notifications. The post records no harm beyond the lost footage, so the canvas cites the post and not a register row.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Under-research observations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>URO-7 and URO-8 at their signing-batch limit.</strong> This release is a signing batch under <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2: it adds incident register rows 92 to 101 and one entry to the regulatory source register. URO-7 (IRQ-SEC-003) and URO-8 (IRQ-SEC-001) therefore passed their signing-batch limit unresolved. Each stays open to its date bound of 8 April 2027, and the ★4 of each canvas stands. A note in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> records this.</li>\n</ul>","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>Correction log — 22 October 2026 (v1.3.4)</h1>\n<p>Changes made in v1.3.4 to text signed at v1.3.3. This version corrects citations against their sources. Where a canvas reads more into a regulatory instrument than the instrument states, and section 1.2 lists that reading, the canvas now says that the reading is its own. The version corrects statements about the standard that the standard's own text contradicts, among them one reviewer-day figure. It gives incident register rows their markers after a new reading of their sources, adds rows 92 to 101 and cites nine of them from the canvases. It adds one entry to the regulatory source register. It names two further method classes in <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3, and it adds one grading rule for all procedures in <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-09.md. An entry that rests on a published source names that source; the other entries rest on the owner's decisions and on the standard's own text. A sentence that names a row or a line and the kind of change declares that change; the row carries the new values. The change logs of the affected canvases and playbooks carry their own v1.3.4 (2026-10-22) lines, which give the detail for each canvas.</p>\n<p>No ★ rating changes. Field 11 changes in 11 canvases, and no rating moves in any of them (sections 1 and 2). <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.1 states that the ★ rating in each canvas carries its anchor, in place of every ★ rating (section 2). Part C of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> names the two tests that the register rates ★4 (section 2). The method class of three procedures changes (section 2). TP-AGT-003 reads Emerging Practice in place of Adapted Industry Practice. TP-AGT-006 and TP-TPR-005 read \"Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice\" in place of \"Mixed\". <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3 adds two entries, \"None established\" and \"Two classes\", and §1 of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> names \"None established\" and states the two-class rule. No Independent Review Question or claim is added, removed or reworded: <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code> do not change. In IRQ-SEC-014 the applicability note of field 1 changes, and the question does not (section 2). <code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code> quotes two questions in full, as the register gives them (section 2). One procedure's reviewer-day figure changes. TP-SEC-002 reads 5.5 in place of 5.0, the sum of its stated parts. The Security domain total moves from 57.0 to 57.5, and the programme total from 455.0 to 455.5 (section 2). No risk-profile value changes: no axis score moves in field 15 or field 16. In IRQ-GOV-016 field 15, one abbreviation is spelled out (section 1.1). No procedure changes its own grade rule. TP-RES-001 §6 removes from its Partial interpretation a statement about the canvas that the canvas does not make (section 2). <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2 adds one grading rule for all procedures: where the facts meet a fail indicator and a partial indicator, the result is Fail. It also states that the procedure's own grade rule decides whether a result is Partial, and that field 14 gives the reporting language and is not a grade rule (section 2). IRQ-GOV-012 field 11 adds a sixth evidence limb, limb (6), to the test, and its ★2 does not move (section 2). No under-research observation is opened or closed. URO-7 and URO-8 stay open past their signing-batch limit (section 6). Two [UNVERIFIED] tags are removed, in field 17 of IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-013, where paragraph pinpoints replace them (section 1.2). In field 17 of IRQ-GOV-008 to IRQ-GOV-012, the [UNVERIFIED] tag stays and lists only the instruments that the canvas cites (section 2). The head of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> adds a note on rows that record no verification and carry no [UNVERIFIED] mark (section 3). The release adds rows 92 to 101 to the incident register (section 4) and one entry to the regulatory source register (section 3). <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2 counts a register addition as a change to the standard's substance. Where a canvas's field 18 Research Basis changes, its \"Research basis last reviewed\" date moves to 22 October 2026. This applies to 35 canvases, among them the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003. No other canvas's date moves. IRQ-MOD-016 adds a worked example to field 10 and keeps its date (section 5). Field 10 of the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003 also changes (section 2). IRQ-SEC-002 changes one evidence item in field 10 and keeps its date (section 1.3).</p>"},{"h":"1. Citations and sources","html":"<h3>1.1 Statements corrected to what the source says</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>McFarlane et al. (2024), <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.</strong> The precedent is stated as the paper gives it (Clinical and Public Health Guidelines 1(4), e12012: abstract, results and conclusion). Its 1,182 recommendations come from 26 NICE COVID-19 living guidelines, not WHO guidelines. The paper finds that some recommendations need an update sooner than others. It argues for a flexible, responsive approach to surveillance, set by the pace of change, in which guideline developers choose which recommendations to keep fully living. The figures of 739 and 335 days stand.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA market study MS18/1.3 (September 2020), IRQ-CON-006 field 18.</strong> The tenure figures are given in pounds, as the study states them, not as percentages (para 3.9, p.13). A motor customer with more than five years' tenure paid on average £85 more than a new customer with the same risk, against a new-business price of £285. A combined buildings-and-contents customer paid £122 more, against £165. The £1.2bn figure carries its base: 6 million policyholders who paid high prices in 2018 (para 1.6). The £4.2bn saving carries its scenario, no change in the intensity of competition (para 5.19).</li>\n<li><strong>Zeka and Johns (2026, Financial Planning Review), IRQ-CON-003 field 18.</strong> The five scenarios are listed as Table 1 of the paper gives them. Illness is not among them. The recommendations varied across tools (abstract), in place of \"conflicting recommendations\". The gender-role bias is placed in one scenario (scenario 2).</li>\n<li><strong>Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan (arXiv:1609.05807), IRQ-CON-001 fields 7 and 9.</strong> The canvas cites the paper for its own result (§1; Theorem 1.1). Calibration within groups and balance for the positive and negative classes cannot all hold at once, except with perfect prediction or equal base rates. The paper does not include demographic parity in that result.</li>\n<li><strong>MemToC (Varlamov et al., arXiv:2608.26295v1), IRQ-AGT-008 field 18 and <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong> The benchmark sets a model's own parametric memory, an elicited closed-book answer, against a tool return of known correctness (abstract; §3.3). It has no stored memory entry and no source-of-truth system. The canvas no longer says that the benchmark measures the construct of its field 11. It names the benchmark as the nearest published benchmark of memory–tool arbitration. The finding and relevance cells of the register row say the same. The full text was read to §4 on 22 October 2026. The ★1 rating is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>GSM1k (Zhang et al., arXiv:2405.00332), IRQ-MOD-011 field 7.</strong> The GSM8K example states what the re-creation of the test found: score drops in some model families (§5.1), and little or no sign of overfitting in frontier models (abstract; conclusion). It had said that frontier models were substantially contaminated.</li>\n<li><strong>Kadavath et al. (2022) is an arXiv preprint.</strong> IRQ-MOD-005 (fields 11 and 21), the ★ line of TP-MOD-005 and the field 21 footer of the exemplar canvas for IRQ-MOD-006 no longer call it peer-reviewed. In IRQ-MOD-005 the peer-reviewed limb rests on Xiong et al. (ICLR 2024) and Tian et al. (EMNLP 2023). In TP-MOD-005 it rests on Xiong et al. (ICLR 2024) and Tian et al. (EMNLP 2023), as in the canvas. In the exemplar canvas it rests on Farquhar et al. (Nature, 2024) and Guo et al. (ICML 2017).</li>\n<li><strong>Doshi-Velez and Kim (arXiv:1702.08608v2).</strong> The evaluation level that the test of IRQ-HUM-007 adapts is named application-grounded (real humans, real tasks; §3.1). It had been named human-grounded, which is the paper's level for simplified tasks (§3.2). The change is made in IRQ-HUM-007 fields 9, 11, 14 and 18, in the confidence and method-class line of TP-HUM-007, and in the TP-HUM-007 row of the domain table of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_HUM.md</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>BCBS 239 (January 2013), IRQ-GOV-016 and TP-GOV-016.</strong> BCBS 239 names no traceability principle. The canvas no longer credits it with a traceability principle, requirement or expectation (fields 9, 17 and 21). The flagging control extends the expectations of BCBS 239 on metadata about the characteristics of the data (Principle 2, para 33) and on documented aggregation processes (Principle 3, para 39). The traceability control rests on the reconciliation of risk data to the bank's sources (para 36(c)). It also rests on the data-lineage expectation of the ECB \"Guide on effective risk data aggregation and risk reporting\" (May 2024, Section 3.4), which field 17 now cites. The guide does not address AI-originated content. Field 17 names the principles of accuracy and integrity, completeness, timeliness and adaptability as Principles 3 to 6. The 2016 date states that global systemically important banks identified in 2011 or 2012 must meet the Principles by January 2016 (para 14). This replaces \"initially binding\" from 1 January 2016 (fields 9, 17 and 20, and the method classification of TP-GOV-016). RDARR is spelled out at its first use in field 15 and in the personnel section of TP-GOV-016.</li>\n<li>The change log of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_GOV.md</code> adds a v1.3.4 line for TP-GOV-016. It records the 2016 date in the method classification and RDARR in full in the personnel section. The ★2 rating and the reviewer-day estimate are unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>NIST AI RMF 1.0 (NIST AI 100-1, January 2023).</strong> IRQ-HUM-001, IRQ-HUM-002 and IRQ-HUM-008 field 9, and the control example for IRQ-HUM-001 in §9 of <code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>, cite the category as MANAGE 4, as NIST AI 100-1 numbers it, in place of MANAGE-4. The control example for IRQ-DAT-007 in §9 of <code>00_Admin/IRQ_TEMPLATE.md</code>, and the example version record in §4 of <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code>, cite the category MEASURE 2 in the same form, in place of MEASURE-2.</li>\n<li><strong>ISAE 3000 (Revised), <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, Layer 2.</strong> The sentence on qualified conclusions keeps para 74 for a material scope limitation or a material misstatement. It adds para 43 for criteria found unsuitable, or subject matter found not appropriate, after the engagement is accepted.</li>\n<li><strong>ISAE 3000 (Revised), <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> Part A.3.</strong> The rule on a later re-rating now applies to a re-rating made because research has moved. The rule cites the treatment of subsequent events in an assurance engagement (ISAE 3000 (Revised), para 61), with ISA 560 and IAS 10 as the audit analogue. It had cited ISA 560 and IAS 10 only. A new sentence states that a rating corrected because it was wrong when the opinion was issued is not research movement. The correction log names such a rating, and the issuer considers whether the conclusion still stands.</li>\n<li><strong>Assurance case and Goal Structuring Notation.</strong> Part I.1 of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> and the Purpose of Component 6 in <code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code> cite two sources: ISO/IEC 15026-2 for the assurance case, and the GSN Community Standard, Version 2, for the notation. They had cited ISO/IEC 15026-2 for Goal Structuring Notation. In <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code> §2 (Design evolution), row 4 says that the practice comes from safety-critical industries. It names the GSN Community Standard, UL 4600 (for autonomous products) and ISO/IEC 15026-2, in place of aviation, nuclear and medical devices. These are the sources that the References &amp; Standards of <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> list.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.2 Regulatory instruments: what they require, and what the canvas reads into them</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PRA SS1/21 (March 2022) and FCA SYSC 15A.</strong> The operational resilience limbs state what the instruments require and expect. A firm must remain within impact tolerance in a severe but plausible disruption (SS1/21 para 4.1; SYSC 15A.2.9R). It sets its tolerances without regard to the cause of disruption (SS1/21 para 3.5). It maps the people, processes, technology, facilities and information that deliver each important business service (SS1/21 para 5.1). It tests regularly (SS1/21 para 6.1; SYSC 15A.5.3R) and acts on the vulnerabilities that mapping and testing find (SS1/21 para 5.3). SS1/21 expects that testing to focus on response and recovery, not on prevention (para 6.1). A firm's communication plans include escalation paths (SS1/21 para 4.7). Neither instrument requires safe degradation or tested escalation paths, and SS1/21 states no containment expectation. SS1/21 notes that there may be circumstances in which continuing to deliver a service through disruption has a more adverse impact than suspending it (para 3.15). FCA guidance treats resuming a degraded service as usually only appropriate where, having regard to the interest of the firm's clients, the soundness, stability and resilience of the UK financial system and the orderly operation of the financial markets, the benefits of resuming it outweigh the negatives of keeping the service unavailable until the issues have been fully remediated and the service is able to be fully restored to its pre-disruption levels (SYSC 15A.2.10G). Each canvas now states its control as its own reading of the regime:</li>\n<li>safe degradation: IRQ-AGT-005 fields 9 and 17, and IRQ-AGT-013 field 17, which also names automated processes;</li>\n<li>mid-plan termination: IRQ-AGT-002 field 9;</li>\n<li>runaway-delegation containment, and disruption from uncontrolled resource consumption: IRQ-AGT-006 fields 9 and 17;</li>\n<li>structural gating of irreversible actions: IRQ-AGT-012 field 17;</li>\n<li>tested escalation: IRQ-GOV-008 fields 9, 17 and 21.</li>\n<li>In field 17 of IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-013, the paragraph pinpoints replace an [UNVERIFIED] tag that said no paragraph pinpoint was confirmed.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008 field 5 states what PRA SS1/23 (April 2026) expects of ongoing monitoring under Principle 4.4(b)(iii). Firms assess whether changes in products, exposures, activities, clients or market conditions should be addressed through model adjustments, recalibration or redevelopment, or by the replacement of the model. The canvas states as its own reading, of that expectation and of the operational resilience regime, that operational signals must inform governance decisions. The text had said that SS1/23 and the operational resilience regime both expect this.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008 fields 9 and 17 state what SS1/23 (April 2026) expects. Under Principle 4.4(b)(iii), firms use ongoing monitoring to assess whether changes in products, exposures, activities, clients or market conditions call for model adjustment, recalibration, redevelopment or replacement. Under Principle 5.2(a), firms consider restrictions on model use when model performance tests show that a significant breach has occurred or is likely. The canvas states as its own reading that monitoring outcomes must inform governance decisions on model status, use restriction or withdrawal. The text had said that SS1/23 requires this. The verification note of field 17 records the read of Principles 4.4 and 5.2.</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring, IRQ-RES-005 fields 5 and 7.</strong> No source states the quoted expectation of \"continuous monitoring with defined triggers\". Field 5 cites the PRA SS1/23 expectations on which the limb rests: firms monitor model performance against thresholds, and apply mitigants once defined triggers and thresholds are breached (SS1/23, April 2026, Principles 4.4(a) and 5.3(a)(ii)). PRA SS1/21 states no monitoring expectation. Field 5 states threshold-triggered response as the canvas's reading of SS1/21 para 4.16, which calls operational resilience a dynamic activity. Field 7 states it as the canvas's reading too.</li>\n<li><strong>PRA SS1/23 (April 2026), pinpoints.</strong></li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-007 fields 9 and 17. Principle 4 is the anchor for the independence of validation from model development and from the people who own the models, with the standing to challenge (4.1(d) and (e)). The validation function recommends to the model approval authority (4.1(b)), and that authority ensures that recommendations for remediation or redevelopment are actioned (Principle 4). SS1/23 does not give the validation function authority to reject use. Restrictions on model use are a decision of the firm under 5.2. The authority to reject deployment is stated as the canvas's extension.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-001 fields 9 and 17 cite Principle 2 (Governance) at 2.4(c), which asks that the firm document who owns each model. This replaces \"governance expectations\".</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-008 field 17 cites Principle 3.5, in place of Principles 2 and 4. It states what 3.5(a) asks: documentation that lets an independent third party replicate parameter estimation and model results. The reconstruction of decisions that depend on retrieval is the canvas's extension.</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-012 field 17 cites Principles 4.4 (model performance monitoring) and 4.3 (process verification), in place of \"Principle 4/5\". Principle 4.3(a) asks firms to confirm that all model components operate effectively. The canvas reads the retrieval layer as one of those components.</li>\n<li><strong>Model inventory, IRQ-TPR-003 fields 17 and 21.</strong> The inventory limb rests on a PRA expectation: banks keep a comprehensive model inventory that includes AI/ML models. The source is SS1/23, as the Bank of England and PRA letter of 22 April 2024 states it (p.10). The FCA AI Update (April 2024) names no inventory, and the canvas no longer attributes the expectation to it. An inventory of all AI use, however delivered, is the canvas's reading.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA AI Update (April 2024), IRQ-AGT-009 field 17.</strong> The Update notes that SYSC 4.1.1R requires robust governance arrangements (para 3.39). It states no change-control expectation. Control of the changes that affect regulated activities is the canvas's reading of SYSC 4.1.1R.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA AI Update (April 2024), IRQ-GOV-005 fields 9, 17 and 21.</strong> The Update sets out how the existing framework, including SYSC and the Consumer Duty, applies to firms' use of AI (paras 3.7 and 4.3). It prescribes no AI policy content. The translation of those obligations into firm-level AI policy is now the canvas's reading. The text had said that the FCA confirms or expects that translation. Field 21 (Test 2) states the same reading.</li>\n<li><strong>UK GDPR.</strong> IRQ-DAT-010 field 17 states what the Articles say. Art. 17(1) gives a right to erasure without undue delay, on the grounds it lists. Art. 30(1) requires a controller to keep a record of its processing activities. Verifiable deletion of all copies, and the recording of a memory store as a processing activity, are the canvas's reading. In <code>V4_Evidence_Catalogue/EVIDENCE_PACK.md</code> §4.3, a Data Protection Impact Assessment applies at any tier where the processing is likely to result in a high risk to individuals (UK GDPR Art. 35(1)), in place of all tiers.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA Consumer Duty, <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>.</strong> In the Operating Envelope Declaration example, the limb on vulnerable customers cites the Consumer Duty, which requires firms to take account of characteristics of vulnerability (PRIN 2A.2.25G). This replaces \"COBS 2.1R protections\". COBS 2.1.1R, the client's best interests rule, does not mention vulnerability. The Consumer Duty row of the Ten Materiality Dimensions table names the four outcomes of the Duty (PRIN 2A.3 to 2A.6) and the treatment of customers with characteristics of vulnerability. This replaces \"needs-based advice\", a term that PRIN 2A does not use.</li>\n<li><strong>FCA PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) and FG22/5, IRQ-CON-010 and TP-CON-010.</strong> PRIN 2A.6.3G(2) provides that appropriate friction included to comply with PRIN 2A.6.2R(3) does not amount to an unreasonable barrier. It gives no figure. FG22/5 ¶9.20 says that what amounts to either \"will depend on the circumstances\", and expects firms to apply judgement. The text had said that the provision places the boundary in the assessor's judgement. The change is made in IRQ-CON-010 field 11, in the ★ statement (§1) and the materiality threshold (§5) of TP-CON-010, and in the disclosure requirement of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_CON.md</code>. The basis cell of the threshold adds FG22/5 ¶9.20. ★1 is unchanged, and the materiality of an asymmetry stays reviewer-set.</li>\n<li><strong>NCSC, \"Managing the cyber risk of agentic AI\".</strong> The quotation on default-deny network access opens with the qualifier of the source, \"Where possible\" (item 4 c). The change is made in IRQ-AGT-003 field 9 and in the row \"Managing the cyber risk of agentic AI\" in section 25 of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> (two places).</li>\n<li><strong>EU AI Act, read in the consolidated text of 27 July 2026 (CELEX 02024R1689-20260727).</strong></li>\n<li>Annex III insurance pricing is limited to life and health insurance, as Annex III point 5(c) states. The change is made in IRQ-DAT-001 field 17, IRQ-GOV-009 fields 9 and 17, IRQ-GOV-011 fields 2, 6 and 7, the headline finding of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_A.md</code>, and the section \"Notable 2025–2026 additions\" of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>.</li>\n<li>2 August 2026 is stated as the general date of application of the Act (Art. 113, second paragraph), and the Article 50 transparency obligations apply from that date. This replaces a date for Article 50 alone (IRQ-DAT-001 and IRQ-SEC-004 field 17).</li>\n<li>The headline finding of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/VERIFICATION_LOG_A.md</code> carries a dated note that says the same. The note adds that providers of systems placed on the market before that date must comply with Art. 50(2) by 2 December 2026 (Art. 111(4)).</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-009 field 17 gives both postponed dates: 2 December 2027 for Annex III systems and 2 August 2028 for Annex I systems (Art. 113, third paragraph, point (c), as amended). It adds the limb of Art. 111(2), as amended. A high-risk system placed on the market or put into service before that date comes under the Regulation only if its design changes significantly from that date. A system intended for use by public authorities must comply by 2 August 2030. This replaces a clause that left uses outside Annex III in scope.</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-001 and IRQ-SEC-004 field 17 state that providers of systems placed on the market before 2 August 2026 must comply with Art. 50(2) by 2 December 2026 (Art. 111(4), as amended). This replaces a date from which Art. 50(2) marking applies. The Date cell of the AI Act row in <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §5 says the same. In <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, the Art 50 worked example of the Dated Forward Obligation Trigger gives 2 August 2026 as the date from which the obligations apply (Art 113). For systems that generate synthetic content and were placed on the market before that date, it gives 2 December 2026 for Art 50(2) (Art 111(4)).</li>\n<li>The Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 row of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §5 adds Art 1(39)(a) of that Regulation, which replaces Art. 111(2). It states the limb that IRQ-AGT-009 field 17 states, with 2 August 2030 for high-risk systems intended for use by public authorities. The row names the amending act and the consolidated text as read on 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>IRQ-HUM-002 field 9 quotes Art. 14(4)(d) in its own words: the overseer must be enabled \"to decide, in any particular situation, not to use the high-risk AI system or to otherwise disregard, override or reverse the output of the high-risk AI system\". This replaces a shortened phrase in quotation marks, \"decide not to use\".</li>\n<li><strong>Field 17 verification notes.</strong> Where a field 17 line now rests on a source read on 22 October 2026 and carries a verification note, the note records that read (a line with no verification note does not gain one):</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-001: the AI Act text;</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-004: the AI Act text and the DSIT AI Cyber Security Code of Practice;</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-009: the AI Act text and the FCA AI Update;</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-013: the SS1/21 and SYSC 15A paragraphs;</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-008 and IRQ-DAT-012: SS1/23;</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-011: the DSIT AI Cyber Security Code of Practice;</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008: the SS1/21 and SYSC 15A paragraphs;</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-010: UK GDPR Articles 17 and 30;</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-009: the AI Act text;</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-003: the Bank of England and PRA letter and the FCA AI Update;</li>\n<li>43 field 17 lines that cite PRA SS1/23: the SS1/23 dates (section 1.4).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.3 Security frameworks cited for what they contain</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>DSIT AI Cyber Security Code of Practice (January 2025) and its Implementation Guide.</strong> The canvases cite the Code for the provisions it contains. The Code has no data-flow mapping principle, no rule on secrets in AI system context, no provision on credential lifetime or revocation, and no principles for agentic systems.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-008 (fields 9, 11, 17, 18 and 21) and the confidence line of TP-SEC-008 cite provision 5.1, an inventory of assets that includes their interdependencies and connectivity. Fields 17 and 18 add provision 5.4, protection of sensitive data against unauthorised access. Fields 9 and 18 cite the example in the Implementation Guide of mapping threats and data flows under provision 3.1. Field 11 no longer names the Code or OWASP LLM02:2025 as the test method, because neither sets one out.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-006 field 17 cites provisions 6.1 and 5.4. It also cites the example that the Implementation Guide gives under provision 6.1: restrict access to system prompts and configuration data.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-011 (fields 9, 11, 17, 18 and 21), the confidence line of TP-SEC-011, IRQ-SEC-004 field 17 and IRQ-SEC-010 field 17 cite provision 2.6. Under that provision, permissions granted to an AI system on other systems are limited to what its function requires and are risk assessed. The Implementation Guide applies that provision to agentic systems. IRQ-SEC-011 field 9 rests the short-lived credential limb on general practice only. Field 11 cites the Code for the scope rule tested, not for the method.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Agentic AI Threats and Mitigations (Version 1.0, February 2025), LLM06:2025 and LLM10:2025.</strong> These sources describe risks and mitigations. None sets out a test method.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-003 field 11, and the ★ line and method classification of TP-AGT-003, cite LLM06:2025 and threat T2 Tool Misuse, in place of test guidance and a test pattern.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-005 field 11 and the method classification of TP-AGT-005 cite the threats and mitigations that the document describes, in place of test patterns.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-006 fields 9 and 11 and the method classification of TP-AGT-006 name the threat as the source names it, T4 Resource Overload, in place of a \"resource-exhaustion category\". They cite LLM10:2025 for mitigation guidance, in place of test guidance.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Machine Learning Security Top 10.</strong> None of its ten items names deserialisation. IRQ-TPR-005 (fields 11 and 21), the confidence line of TP-TPR-005 and the domain overview of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code> cite it for supply-chain risk only. Field 21 cites ML06:2023, whose model-hub scenario has malicious code from a downloaded model run in the organisation's environment. The ★3 of the component is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP LLM03:2025 Supply Chain and ML06:2023 ML Supply Chain Attacks.</strong> Neither entry's prevention and mitigation guidance names a serialisation format (LLM03:2025, Prevention and Mitigation Strategies; ML06:2023, How to Prevent). The serialisation-format control is now stated as the canvas's own.</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-005 field 9 and IRQ-SEC-009 field 9 cite LLM03:2025 for models from verifiable sources, with signing and file hashes to confirm model integrity, and say that the entry's prevention and mitigation strategies name no serialisation format. IRQ-TPR-005 field 9 had cited the entry for unsafe model serialisation formats.</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-005 field 18 says that the two entries document the model supply-chain and artefact-integrity risks, and that the prevention guidance of neither names serialisation formats. It had said that they document the unsafe-serialisation risk. Both entries were read again on 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>IRQ-TPR-005 fields 11 and 14 state that limb (c) of the test, on serialisation formats, has no OWASP anchor. Field 14 anchors the ★3 component on the supply-chain risk of ML06:2023. The ★3 of the component and the ★2 of the canvas are unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_SEC.md</code>.</strong> The note on OWASP entry numbers says that the canvas files of the domain (the thirteen files CANVASES_SEC_001.md to CANVASES_SEC_014.md; IRQ-SEC-003 is in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>) cite the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications in its 2025 edition, with the <code>:2025</code> suffix or with the edition named in words. A 2026 entry number appears only as a read-across. The note had said that every citation in the file carries the suffix. The domain introduction says that direct injection/jailbreak and extraction testing generally rate ★3–★4 on published sources (OWASP, MITRE ATLAS). It also says that the basis of the ★4 of IRQ-SEC-001 is under review as URO-8. It had called these sources published methodology. It had also named NIST AI 100-2, which no canvas for those tests cites.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-002 field 10.</strong> The L4 evidence is an independent red-team report whose technique coverage draws on published OWASP and MITRE ATLAS sources. It had said that the coverage is scoped per OWASP/MITRE methodology. ★3 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, Tier 2.</strong> The bullet on benchmarked test methods names a published methodology, such as an OWASP testing guide or a NIST SP 800-53 control assessment, and refers to <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3. This replaces \"(OWASP, NIST)\".</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.4 Dates and editions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ARP 4761.</strong> SAE International issued ARP 4761 in December 1996 and revised it as ARP4761A in December 2023 (SAE Mobilus, the ARP4761 and ARP4761A pages). IRQ-GOV-012 field 20 entry [5] is dated 1996, in place of 2010, a year that no edition carries, and names the revision. The ARP 4761 entry under References &amp; Standards in <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code> gives both dates.</li>\n<li><strong>OWASP Agentic Security Initiative threat model.</strong> Its cover gives Version 1.0, February 2025. The ★2 rationale of IRQ-SEC-004 field 11 and the pitfalls section of TP-SEC-004 give that date. They had said that the threat model was \"under a year old\", which the canvas's own dates contradict.</li>\n<li><strong>PRA SS1/23, the dates of the April 2026 version.</strong> The cover of the April 2026 version gives \"16 April 2026 (updating 17 May 2023)\". The PRA's page for the version gives \"Published 23 April 2026. Effective from 23 April 2026.\" The text of 16 April 2026 keeps para 1.5, under which the policy came into effect on 17 May 2024. Each citation of the April 2026 version keeps its cover date and adds that the version was published and took effect on 23 April 2026.</li>\n<li>The limb \"published and effective 23 April 2026\" is added at 140 sites in fields 9, 17 and 20 of 69 canvases: IRQ-AGT-001 to IRQ-AGT-013; IRQ-DAT-001, IRQ-DAT-003 to IRQ-DAT-008 and IRQ-DAT-011 to IRQ-DAT-014; IRQ-GOV-001 to IRQ-GOV-012; IRQ-MOD-001 to IRQ-MOD-005 and IRQ-MOD-007 to IRQ-MOD-016; IRQ-RES-005 and IRQ-RES-006; IRQ-SEC-001, IRQ-SEC-002, IRQ-SEC-004 to IRQ-SEC-010, IRQ-SEC-013 and IRQ-SEC-014; IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-TPR-004 and IRQ-TPR-005; and the exemplar canvases for IRQ-SEC-003 and IRQ-MOD-006.</li>\n<li>At 51 of these sites, in field 17, field 20 or both, the citation also gives the policy as in effect since 17 May 2024 (para 1.5). This replaces a single effective date of May 2024 or 17 May 2024. These sites are in 32 canvases: IRQ-AGT-001; IRQ-DAT-001 and IRQ-DAT-014; IRQ-GOV-001 to IRQ-GOV-012; IRQ-MOD-001 to IRQ-MOD-005 and IRQ-MOD-007 to IRQ-MOD-016; and the exemplar canvases for IRQ-SEC-003 and IRQ-MOD-006.</li>\n<li>Where a field 17 line that gains the limb \"published and effective 23 April 2026\" carries a verification note, the note records that the SS1/23 dates were read on 22 October 2026 (43 lines). The notes of IRQ-DAT-008 and IRQ-DAT-012 already record a read of SS1/23 on that date. A changed line with no verification note does not gain one.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-008 field 20 no longer repeats the publication date after \"amended by LIAF01/26\".</li>\n<li><code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.3 gives the April 2026 version as \"published and effective 23 April 2026\", in place of \"amendment published 23 April 2026\".</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-014 keeps \"effective 17 May 2024\" for the May 2023 version, which the PRA's page gives as effective from that date. Its citations of the April 2026 version already gave the limb.</li>\n<li><strong>PoisonedRAG (Zou et al.).</strong> The paper is an arXiv preprint of 2024 (arXiv:2402.07867), published in the 34th USENIX Security Symposium (USENIX Security 25), 2025, pp. 3827–3844 (the USENIX record of the paper). IRQ-SEC-005 fields 18 and 20, and the PoisonedRAG row of <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>, add the USENIX publication. Fields 18 and 20 of the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003 give the year 2024 and the arXiv number, with the 2025 USENIX publication, in place of the year 2025. Field 21 of IRQ-SEC-005 calls the research peer-reviewed, and the USENIX publication is the source of that status.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>1.5 Addresses</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ-HUM-008 field 20, entry [4].</strong> The entry gives the FCA Handbook addresses of DISP and PRIN 2A, in place of the Handbook home page.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-DAT-001 field 20, entry [5].</strong> The entry states that its original address redirected to an unrelated page when read on 22 October 2026, and points to the archived copy of the release.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"2. Statements about the standard","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code>, Cross-Domain Links.</strong> The two examples name links that the canvases carry in field 19 and that <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> records. IRQ-MOD-013 links to IRQ-RES-004 (G.2, Containment &amp; Recovery), because a rollback of prompts and configuration is part of a full rollback of behavioural components. This replaces IRQ-HUM-007. IRQ-DAT-007 links to IRQ-SEC-003, in place of IRQ-SEC-004.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-014 field 1.</strong> The applicability note gives the Y for generative systems as the reason why the question is not restricted to agentic deployments. It had given the N on the third limb, which is the predictive limb.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-HUM-009.</strong> The confidence line marks two of the six expected controls as Emerging Practice, in place of two of five. The canvas added the EU AI Act Article 86 control at v1.3.3.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-RES-001 §6.</strong> The Partial interpretation no longer says that the canvas names it the most common outcome. The canvas ranks no outcome as the most common.</li>\n<li><strong>Domain overviews of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_HUM.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code> and <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code>.</strong> The overviews no longer state that assurance objectives and pass, fail and partial indicators are carried across unchanged. ★ ratings are carried unchanged.</li>\n<li>Each of the three overviews states that assurance objectives and pass and fail indicators are restated from the canvases. Some objectives and some indicators keep the canvas's words, and the rest are reworded. Where a procedure and its canvas differ, the canvas governs (<code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2). The overviews no longer name partial indicators, in line with §2 of the overview as this release states it.</li>\n<li>In <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code>, a result maps to the canvas's pass and fail indicators and its reporting language, in place of pass/fail/partial indicators. Field 11 is described as naming a published methodology where one exists, because TP-SEC-012 runs no technical test.</li>\n<li><strong>Technical limbs in staging, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code>.</strong> The domain overview and operating rule 1 name TP-TPR-005 with TP-TPR-004 and TP-TPR-006 as procedures with a technical limb run in isolated staging. TP-TPR-005's limb is its bounded behavioural probe.</li>\n<li><strong>Reviewer-days.</strong> In the time/effort estimate of TP-SEC-002 (<code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_002.md</code>), the estimate is 5.5 reviewer-days, the sum of its stated parts (0.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 + 0.5 + 0.5 + 1.0), in place of 5.0. <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC.md</code> gives the Security domain total as 57.5 reviewer-days, in place of 57.0, in its total indicative effort and in its effort table. <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §9 gives the programme total as 455.5 reviewer-days, in place of 455.0, and its calibration note follows. No ★ rating or test step changes.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_DAT.md</code>, shared test-environment setup.</strong> The environment supports twelve of the original thirteen procedures, in place of eleven, as the component table lists them (all but TP-DAT-003).</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-010 field 20, entry [4].</strong> The entry says that the paper of Zac et al. was read at the primary on 3 October 2026, as field 18 records. It had said that the paper was not read at the primary.</li>\n<li><strong>Method classes, <code>00_Admin/METHODOLOGY_STANDARDS.md</code> §3.</strong> Two entries are added. \"None established\" means that no published testing methodology exists for the question; <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.4 keeps this method class distinct from a control marked \"Emerging Practice\". \"Two classes\" lets a label name two of the classes as \"A / B\", where the parts of a test fall in different classes, and the procedure states which part falls in which class. The standing rule on honesty of method in §1 of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> names \"None established\" and states the two-class rule.</li>\n<li>TP-AGT-003: the method classification reads Emerging Practice, in place of Adapted Industry Practice. Its sources, OWASP LLM06:2025 mitigation guidance and threat T2, set out no test method (section 1.3). ★2 is unchanged.</li>\n<li>TP-AGT-006 and TP-TPR-005: the method class reads \"Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice\", in place of \"Mixed\". The TP-TPR-005 row of the domain table in <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_TPR.md</code> reads the same. The text of each procedure states its two parts. ★2 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Grading, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §2.</strong> The pass and fail positions originate in canvas fields 12 and 13. Field 14 gives the reporting language for each result and is not a grade rule. The procedure's own grade rule decides whether a result is Partial. Where the facts meet a fail indicator and a partial indicator, the result is Fail. This replaces the statement that all three positions originate in fields 12, 13 and 14.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §1, In scope.</strong> The statement adds that, for a ★1 or ★2 procedure, two reviewers may still design the test differently or reach different conclusions (§8).</li>\n<li><strong>Compiled lines.</strong> The heads of <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> and of the AGT, CON, DAT, MOD and RES domain playbooks point to the change log for later changes.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_DAT.md</code>, domain-level evidence maturity caveat.</strong> Input drift (TP-DAT-011) is placed at ★3, and retrieval and embedding drift (TP-DAT-012) at ★2, as the canvases rate them and as the effort summary lists them. The caveat had placed all drift testing at ★3. No ★ rating moves.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-RES-001 §3.</strong> The canvas is said to identify the untrusted-output trigger as a weak point whose trigger is often left undefined. It had been called near-universal.</li>\n<li><strong>TP-AGT-012.</strong> The ★ statement, the method classification and the canvas-limitation pitfall refer to two published worked examples of layered gate design, as the canvas does. These are the OpenAI Operator system card (23 January 2025) and Meta's Muse agent security disclosure (8 September 2026). They had referred to one. ★2 and the method class are unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-012 fields 11, 14 and 21.</strong> The test adds evidence limb (6): the provider's release notes and the firm's version-pinning records for the period, reviewed for any capability that the model or platform gained without a change made by the firm. Evidence limb (2) also names the customer segments actually served, checked against the declared segment, as step 4 of TP-GOV-012 does. Each vector of the six-vector taxonomy that the ★2 rationale names now has an evidence limb, as step 7 of TP-GOV-012 already has. The pass statement of field 14 names the customer segment and silent capability among the tested vectors. The evidence list of Test 3 in the field 21 footer names segment records, the provider's release notes and the firm's version-pinning records. Each list now has one item for each of the six evidence limbs of field 11. ★2 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>Exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003, field 10.</strong> The red-team payload classes are the five that field 11 names: instruction override, goal hijack, tool-invocation, data-exfiltration, and delayed or conditional triggers. Persona-switch, which field 11 does not name, is removed.</li>\n<li><strong>Exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003, field 14.</strong> The pass statement says that testing draws on OWASP LLM01 (2025) guidance, under review as URO-7. It had said that testing follows the LLM01 methodology, which URO-7 records that LLM01:2025 does not give. ★4 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-001 field 14.</strong> The pass statement says that testing draws on OWASP LLM01 (2025) guidance, under review as URO-8, and on MITRE ATLAS AML.T0051 case studies. It had said that testing follows the LLM01 and MITRE ATLAS methodology. URO-8 records that LLM01:2025 gives no test method. Field 11 names the MITRE ATLAS source as AML.T0051 case studies. ★4 is unchanged.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-GOV-008 to IRQ-GOV-012, field 17.</strong> The [UNVERIFIED] tag on section pinpoints lists only the instruments that each canvas cites. Each new list is a part of the old list, and no instrument is added.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ID_SCHEME.md</code>, examples of correct allocation.</strong> The examples for IRQ-MOD-015 and IRQ-HUM-004 quote each question in full, as <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/IRQ_REGISTER.md</code> gives it, in place of a shortened form. The IRQ-MOD-015 example adds the four limbs of the question, from \"known bias classes\" to \"correlated error assessed\". The IRQ-HUM-004 example adds \"with oversight capacity dimensioned against AI throughput and reconstructable behaviour\".</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, Governance.</strong> A sentence says that the headings number the four layers Layer 2, Layer 3, Layer 4 and Final Layer, and that there is no Layer 1.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ENGAGEMENT_MODEL.md</code>, Phase 5, Gate 5 (Opinion Drafted &amp; Peer-Reviewed).</strong> The first criterion requires that the opinion is signed and that the signer's independence is confirmed. It no longer refers to an authorised signer. <code>00_Admin/OWNERSHIP_AND_LICENCE.md</code> states that no person or body is authorised to certify, accredit or approve anything against FAIAS.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>Part H.1 is the Regulatory Alignment Summary, in place of the Compliance Status Summary. Its status column reads Aligned, Conditionally aligned or Not aligned, in place of the compliance terms. Its interpretation states that the table records the reviewer's view of alignment and is not a determination of compliance.</li>\n<li>Part C states that the ★ values in its table are illustrative and are not the register's. At this version the two tests rated ★4, IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003, support CLM-09 only, so no other claim can show ★4.</li>\n<li>The heading of Part J.4 labels its counts as those of the illustrative engagement of Part J.3, not the register's.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md</code>.</strong> In Component 6, Part B, the sub-claims are a separate, illustrative argument, not the core claims of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/CLAIMS_REGISTER.md</code>, and Part C of the opinion reports against the register's claims. The Part H outline of Component 7 uses the alignment terms of <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code> Part H.1.</li>\n<li><strong><code>README.md</code>, the bullet on the correction pass of 29 July 2026.</strong> The pointer names <code>CANVASES_CON_003.md</code>, <code>CANVASES_CON_005.md</code>, <code>CANVASES_CON_006.md</code> and <code>CANVASES_CON_008.md</code>, whose Independent Test states each rating, in place of the domain file <code>CANVASES_CON.md</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-MOD-010, change log.</strong> A note says that the v1.2.0 entry is dated 2026-09-14, the date of its erratum. Every other v1.2.0 entry in the standard is dated 2026-09-12, and v1.2.0 was signed on 15 September 2026. The dated entry stays as written.</li>\n<li><strong><code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.1 (Version-anchored ratings).</strong> The rule states that the ★ rating in each canvas carries its anchor inside the rating artefact, in place of every ★ rating. Field 11 of each of the 109 canvases carries the anchor, \"as at FAIAS\" with the version and its date. The ★ lines of the test procedures carry the rating from the canvas, with no anchor. No ★ rating moves.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"3. Source registers","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>ISO/IEC 27090 (<code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>; <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>).</strong> The standard is published. The Date cell of the register row reads \"International Standard published (stage 60.60), Edition 1, publication date 2026-10 (read by the maintainer 22 Oct 2026)\", and the Type cell reads \"Standard\". The Relevance cell makes the row a crosswalk source for FAIAS Security, cited at the level of the standard, with no clause mapped, in place of a watchlist entry. Its verification note adds the read of 22 October 2026 at the ISO catalogue record: stage 60.60, Edition 1 and publication date 2026-10. The record of the reads of 29 July and 7 October 2026 stays. In §4 (Maintenance model) of <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>, the \"Known-aging content\" bullet no longer names ISO/IEC 27090 among in-flight standards.</li>\n<li><strong>CFPB, \"Chatbots in consumer finance\" (6 June 2023), <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §16.</strong> An entry is added after the entry for the CFPB circular. It describes the report as the CFPB's own research, not a rule or guidance. It records complaints about \"doom loops\" with no \"offramp to a human customer service representative\". The report states that the limits of these chatbots \"may not be apparent to customers when they are initially signing up\". It also states that their deployment \"may also be an intentional choice by entities seeking to grow revenue or minimize write offs\". The entry makes the report a US supervisory signal for IRQ-CON-010. The report names firms as examples of chatbot adoption and technology, names no firm as the subject of the complaints or of these practices, and measures no asymmetry. The verification note records that the whole report was read at the Internet Archive's capture of the publisher's PDF. The additions line at the head of the register and the Summary record the addition: 163 entries + 1 = 164. The regulatory register row of <code>README.md</code> gives 164 register entries, in place of 163.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code>.</strong> The row \"Expanding Daybreak as the Cyber Defense Window Narrows\" gives the completion rates as the page reports them, on advanced cybersecurity requests that include exploit-chain development and authentication bypass. The rates are 95.0% for GPT-5.6-Cyber, available through Daybreak Red, 1.5% for GPT-5.6 Sol, and 2.0% for GPT-5.6 Sol with Daybreak Blue access. This replaces a 95.0% against 1.5% differential tied to Daybreak Blue. The \"Path to Astra\" row quotes the safeguards sentence whole, from \"Based on retrospective testing\". It no longer says that the control was not tested against the event.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong> The MEMOREPAIR row counts the paper as one of the three independent 2026 author groups that IRQ-RES-004 field 18 counts, with ChronoMem and Yu et al. It says that field 18 cites the paper for the cascade mechanism, in place of \"a fourth author group\" and a cascade mechanism asserted without a citation. The row of Mohan et al. (ARIES) says that the paper anchors the generic limb of IRQ-RES-004, which v1.2.0 re-anchored from \"established practice\".</li>\n<li><strong><code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>, IRQ-MOD-016.</strong> The evidence cell attributes the evaluation-awareness rates of 41.1% (xhigh) and 50.6% (max reasoning effort) to Apollo Research's measurement, as OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra System Card reports it (§8.8.1). It had said that the publishers measured them.</li>\n<li><strong>NIST control overlays (<code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §10; <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code>).</strong> On 20 October 2026 NIST renamed the project SP 800-53 Control Overlays for Super Intelligence Systems (COSIS). Documents issued before the rename were not altered and keep the old name (the NIST project page, read 22 October 2026). The register row gives the new title with the former name, Control Overlays for Securing AI Systems (COSAiS), and the new address. Its Date cell names the draft of 8 January 2026 as the latest draft, and adds the rename. A dated note after the note of 7 October 2026 records the rename. The \"Known-aging content\" bullet of <code>00_Admin/DEVELOPMENT_APPROACH.md</code> §4 names NIST COSIS, named COSAiS until 20 October 2026.</li>\n<li><strong>Other rows of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>Head: a dated note says that a row that records a verification gives it and its date in the row. A row with no recorded verification and no [UNVERIFIED] mark records none. The absence of the mark does not show that the row was verified.</li>\n<li>§4, the row for the White Paper and the government response: the address cell gives the addresses of CP 815 and of CP 1019, the government response of 6 February 2024. It had given the White Paper address only.</li>\n<li>§4, the row for the ICO Code and the ADM guidance: a currency note of 22 October 2026 says that the ICO plans page no longer lists the update of the Automated Decision Making (ADM) and Profiling Guidance. An Internet Archive capture of the page of 4 October 2026 still lists it, with \"Winter 2026\". The row now carries no target date for that guidance, and the note of 7 October 2026 stays as the record of its read.</li>\n<li>§12, the row for OWASP Agentic AI: Threats and Mitigations: the currency note names the two threats that the standard cites by number, T2 Tool Misuse and T4 Resource Overload. Each carries the same number and name in v1.0 and v1.1, so the citations are unaffected. The note had said that no entry was cited by number.</li>\n<li>§13, the MITRE ATLAS v2026.08 row: a currency note of 22 October 2026 says that release v2026.09, published 15 September 2026, supersedes the pin, and gives its counts from its release notes. The pin does not move. The one technique identifier that the standard cites, AML.T0051, rests on the v2026.08 data, and the ATLAS counts cited at IRQ-SEC-013 carry their own release date.</li>\n<li>§27, the Bank of Japan FY2026 survey row: the row quotes the summary finding, \"Over 90 percent of financial institutions are using or trialing GenAI\". It then quotes in full the sentence of the annex body that gives the base of that figure. About 80 percent of the institutions use GenAI, and over 90 percent when those under trial are included. The row had quoted only the end of that sentence. The verification note records the read of 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>§28, the CWA 18398:2026 row: the first quoted sentence is labelled the summary statement and the second the mission, as the role profile at Annex B §5.4 labels them. The row had labelled both sentences the mission. The verification note records the read of 22 October 2026.</li>\n<li>Summary: the figure 105 is the total without the ten watch items entered on 25 August 2026. The three watchlist entries already within the 87 stay counted.</li>\n<li>Summary: the paragraph on the addition of 22 October 2026 names the ten existing entries that were rewritten and are not counted as additions. It had said that no existing entry was rewritten on that date.</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>The Wu and Xiao row links to version 1 of the paper, which the row cites and quotes. A note says that version 2 of 21 October 2026 gives the paper a new title (the arXiv record, read 22 October 2026).</li>\n<li>The Xu row quotes the paper's own words, \"one trace cannot decide them\" (arXiv 2609.07162v1). It had quoted \"a single trace cannot decide this\".</li>\n<li>The AI Risk Repository row adds a currency note. The version of August 2024 lists 777 risks from 43 taxonomies. The current version, v3 of 5 May 2026, analyses 74 frameworks with 1,725 distinct risks and is published in Patterns (Cell Press, 2026) (the arXiv record, read 22 October 2026).</li>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code>.</strong></li>\n<li>The row \"Improving our alignment and security efforts\" states that an independent review with METR is planned, as the page states it (\"We are also planning to work with METR for an independent review\"). It had said that the review was commissioned. The entry of 9 September 2026 records the signed agreement.</li>\n<li>The Date cell of the row \"Introducing Claude Fable 5.1 and Claude Mythos 5.1\" says that the page prints the month and year only. It had said that the page prints no date.</li>\n<li><strong>Heads of the registers.</strong> The Compiled line of <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code>, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_LABS.md</code> and <code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code> says that the register is revised in later releases.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"4. Incident register","html":"<h3>4.1 Rows 48, 61, 71 and 79: the primary marker</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code>.</strong> Rows 48, 61, 71 and 79 carry [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] in place of the secondary form. This applies the decision of 8 October 2026, which the text signed at v1.3.3 did not carry. Each row rests in part on a party's own document or statement. These are the affected user's own posts on X (row 48), the company's own Form 10-Q (row 61), the plaintiffs' own pleading (row 71) and the vendor's own CVE record (row 79). Those documents were read again on 22 October 2026. The source type that the secondary form named stands in each Source cell before the marker. The note of 9 October 2026 at the foot of the register now counts rows 47 to 91 as 30 rows with [primary verified 7 Oct 2026], these four rows, and 11 rows with the secondary form. A dated note at the foot records the change.</li>\n<li><strong>Canvases.</strong> IRQ-AGT-002 (row 48), IRQ-DAT-011 (row 61), IRQ-HUM-002 (row 71), IRQ-SEC-014 (row 79) and the exemplar canvas for IRQ-SEC-003 (row 79) no longer call these rows secondary rows in field 18. Each citation keeps the limit it states: the third-party transcript for the machine-learning detail of row 61, the pleading of row 71, and the independent case study for the mechanism of row 79. The v1.3.3 change-log lines of these canvases stay as records. No ★ rating, claim criticality, severity value or deliberate gap changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.2 Rows read again on 22 October 2026</h3>\n<p>The legend now names the notes of 9 and 22 October 2026 at the foot of the register. A note of 22 October 2026 at the foot lists each result below. Rows 4 and 22 were searched again, no primary was located, and they keep their marker. Row 17 keeps its marker.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Primary marker on the issuing party's own document.</strong></li>\n<li>Row 1: the failure mode follows the decision in Moffatt v. Air Canada, 2024 BCCRT 149 (Civil Resolution Tribunal, 14 February 2024, paras 14 to 17 and 27). The decision records that Air Canada gave no information about the nature of its chatbot. The words \"separate legal entity\" are the tribunal's reading of the airline's defence.</li>\n<li>Row 15: SEC press release 2024-36 (18 March 2024). The charges are settled charges of false and misleading statements about the use of AI, made in SEC filings, a press release, websites and social media. \"AI washing\" is the label that the SEC's Chair and its Director of Enforcement used. The firms neither admitted nor denied the findings.</li>\n<li>Row 27: Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme, Report, Volume 1 (7 July 2023, pp. v and x). The Federal Court declaration of 2019 was made with the Government's consent.</li>\n<li>Row 29: National Audit Office, HC 2144 (24 May 2019). The classification was ETS's voice-recognition software, with each result reviewed by two ETS human listeners and a further ETS review of the patterns at each test centre (Summary para 2). At least 11,356 people associated with an invalid or questionable result had left the UK by March 2019, some 2,468 of them by enforced removal (para 3.27). The row gives the two caveats of the NAO: people may have left for other reasons, and the numbers may be underestimates.</li>\n<li>Row 32: one sentence from the same source is added for balance (the risk report, p.148).</li>\n<li>Row 37: the gains from stolen AI credentials are attributed to the general statement of the report (p.30), not to the actor of the row.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary marker for the user's own post on X.</strong> The decision of 22 October 2026 applies to rows 3 and 10 the decision of 8 October 2026. Row 3 adds the customer's instruction to the bot to \"disregard any rules\", from the customer's own screenshots. Row 10 adds the user's instruction to the bot, from the user's own screenshots. The firm's side of each row rests on reporting, which its Source cell names. The note of 19 September 2026 stays true of the report it describes.</li>\n<li><strong>Primary marker for first-party limbs.</strong></li>\n<li>Row 11: the harm is given in NEDA's own words, from NEDA's own post of 30 May 2023, which the note of 26 September 2026 recorded as unread. The dieting content and the vendor's role are attributed to the press.</li>\n<li>Row 16: the Hong Kong Government's written reply in the Legislative Council (LCQ9, 26 June 2024, reply part (1)) is the source. It records a pre-recorded video conference and payment instructions given over instant messaging. It records a loss of about HK$200 million to five local bank accounts. The row also cites the firm's own report (Arup, Annual and ESG Report FY2023/24, p.7). The 15 transfers and the firm's identity are attributed to the press.</li>\n<li>Row 21: the demonstration is dated to Google's own post of 6 February 2023, and NASA's page dates the first image of an exoplanet to 2004. The fall in market value is attributed to the press.</li>\n<li>Row 28: the row gives the figures of the agency's own review (Michigan Talent Investment Agency, 11 August 2017). Of 62,784 unappealed fraud cases (October 2013 to August 2015), 40,195 were resolved by the computer program, and 85% of those findings were later reversed. 22,589 were referred to an investigator, and 44% of those were reversed. The agency says that most reversals were overpayments without intent to defraud. \"No human review\" is limited to the cases that the program resolved. The 93% figure stays as pleaded only. The pleadings attribute it to the state's audit office, whose MiDAS audit states that it did not review the identification of fraudulent claims (report 641-0593-15, February 2016, p.30). The 85% figure that the correction log of 19 September 2026 removed as unsourced is in the agency's review.</li>\n<li><strong>Secondary form, with the source type after the dash.</strong></li>\n<li>Row 19: the word \"RAG\" is removed from the failure mode and the domain cell, because no source read states the chatbot's architecture. The row cites The Markup (29 March 2024) and the city's release of 16 October 2023.</li>\n<li>Row 26: the dataset is described as holding links, not images, as LAION states (19 December 2023). The finding of the Stanford Internet Observatory (19 December 2023) is read at its archived copy.</li>\n<li>Row 33: the CVSS version is named, v4.0 9.4, and the CVE record also carries v3.1 9.6.</li>\n<li>Row 34: the statement that the chain is not prompt injection is marked as the register's own reading. The source does not use the term.</li>\n<li>Row 35: the figures for the Cursor-driven subset are attributed to Gambit Security (27 August 2026), whose report is their origin. The note of 12 September 2026 stays as the dated record.</li>\n<li>Row 36: one sentence from the same source is added for balance (the BIS FSI paper, Box A).</li>\n<li><strong>Rows 47 to 91 read again.</strong> Rows 50 and 80 add a later document of the vendor that does not refer to the disclosure (GitHub changelog, 10 December 2025; Salesforce release note on Trusted URL enforcement, from 8 September 2025). Both keep the secondary form. Row 64 is now read in full, at the published version in eScholarship, and keeps the secondary form. Row 71 adds the defendant's filed answer (Doc. 57, filed 2 May 2025, ECF pp.2, 3 and 6). Row 79 gives Microsoft's own record (Security Update Guide, CVE-2025-32711, revision 1.2, 20 February 2026) as the source of its fix and exploitation limbs. Row 86 adds the vendor's hardening guide, read at an Internet Archive capture of 27 June 2024. Row 88 adds the scanner changes of picklescan pull requests #33 and #35. The note of 9 October 2026 gives the new source types of rows 64 and 86. Its counts for rows 47 to 91 stay true.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.3 Rows 92 to 101 added</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Row 92</strong>, IBM Financial Transaction Manager, CVE-2026-18875: an unauthenticated write path into the runbook store of an AI agent server in payments software (IBM security bulletin, first published 21 September 2026). It is a vendor's record of a defect in shipped software, entered on the inclusion note of 12 September 2026. It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-SEC-005.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 93</strong>, a campaign against South Korean financial organisations with an agentic penetration-testing tool (CrowdStrike, 7 October 2026; Yonhap, 1 October 2026; the FSC and FSS consumer alert of 6 October 2026). It carries the secondary form and states the attribution grade in words. It carries no personal detail of the suspected actor, and no canvas cites it.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 94</strong>, Railway: an agent deleted a production volume with an account-scoped API token that it found on the developer's machine (Railway, 29 April 2026; changelog of 1 May 2026). It rests on the platform provider's own account, which names neither the customer nor the agent. It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-AGT-003 and IRQ-SEC-011.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 95</strong>, Open WebUI, CVE-2026-45398: retrieval endpoints that did not apply the access control for knowledge-base collections (project advisory GHSA-4g37-7p2c-38r9, 10 May 2026). It rests on the project's own advisory, carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026], and supports IRQ-DAT-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 96</strong>, the ACCEPT deskilling study: the adenoma detection rate of colonoscopy done without AI fell after four centres introduced AI (Budzyń et al., Lancet Gastroenterology &amp; Hepatology 10(10):896–903, October 2025). It is labelled an observational study of a deployed practice, not a harm event. It carries the secondary form for a peer-reviewed study read at its abstract, and supports IRQ-HUM-003.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 97</strong>, Oversight Board case 2020-004-IG-UA (28 January 2021): an automated classifier removed a post, and the company had stated that not all appeals receive human review. The row rests on the Oversight Board's own decision, which is not a court or a regulator. It carries the secondary form, with the source type after the dash: an oversight body's own decision, with the company's statements as the Board records them. It supports IRQ-HUM-008.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 98</strong>, Claude 3.7 Sonnet special-casing of tests, from the developer's system card (sections 6 and 6.1, p.22). It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-MOD-007 and IRQ-MOD-008.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 99</strong>, OpenAI o3: reward hacking of a scoring function and false reports by the model about its actions, from the developer's system card (16 April 2025, sections 3.9.1 and 3.9.2). The outside evaluators tested the models before release, with early checkpoints and the final launch candidates (section 3.9), and METR's findings concern earlier checkpoints. The fabricated-action limb rests on an outside evaluator's report (Transluce, 16 April 2025), which the Source cell names. It carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026] and supports IRQ-MOD-007, IRQ-MOD-008 and IRQ-MOD-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 100</strong>, the LiteLLM 1.107.0 supply-chain compromise of 21 October 2026. A poisoned release of an open-source AI gateway was signed and published through the developer's own release pipeline (LiteLLM, account last updated 21 October 2026, 20:30 UTC; OSV MAL-2026-2144; PyPI). A second limb gives three LiteLLM entries in CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue (CVE-2026-42208, CVE-2026-42271 and CVE-2026-59822; catalogue version 2026.10.21). The stealer's database query and the later use of stolen credentials rest on a security firm's report (Wiz, 21 October 2026), which the Source cell names. The root cause is not confirmed, and the row gives each of the developer's statements at the strength that the developer gives it. The row is entered on the inclusion note of 12 September 2026, carries [primary verified 22 Oct 2026], and supports IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-011.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 101</strong>, Libra Internet Bank: the bank's customer-service assistant, Lia, called a customer „idiotule” (idiot) in a chat, and the bank apologised and added prohibitions. The exchange rests on a press report of the image of the chat that the customer published (Wall-Street.ro, 18 September 2026). The bank's account of the cause, its apology and its added prohibitions rest on the bank's own statement on its LinkedIn company page, headed „Lia (prin OpenAI)”, which does not quote the reply. The sources do not date the exchange. The row quotes both sources in Romanian, each quotation with the standard's own translation. It carries the secondary form, with the source of each limb after the dash. It is in financial services and supports IRQ-CON-009.</li>\n<li><strong>Inclusion note of 22 October 2026.</strong> A note at the head of the register admits a peer-reviewed observational study of a deployed practice, with no harm event. The condition is that the study measures the quantity that an Independent Review Question asks about. Such a row is labelled in its Incident cell. Row 96 is the first.</li>\n<li><strong>Notes at the foot.</strong> The notes of 22 October 2026 give the source and the marker of each new row. Rows 94 to 100 are outside financial services, as their Institution cells say. Row 101 is in financial services, and its note states why it carries the secondary form. No IRQ star rating, claim criticality, severity value or deliberate gap changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.4 Rows linked to further questions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>IRQ(s) supported.</strong> The cells of rows 2, 17, 47, 56, 63, 66, 69 and 70 name further questions whose canvases now cite the rows: row 2, IRQ-GOV-008; row 17, IRQ-AGT-003; row 47, IRQ-AGT-010; row 56, IRQ-GOV-011; row 63, IRQ-MOD-016 and IRQ-RES-004; row 66, IRQ-GOV-004 and IRQ-TPR-003; row 69, IRQ-HUM-007; row 70, IRQ-HUM-008.</li>\n<li><strong>Text added at the cited sources.</strong> Row 2 adds the 97 automated e-mails that were not acted upon before the market opened (SEC order, Release No. 34-70694, para 19). Row 17 adds the customer's later account of the agent's access to production databases (SaaStr, 2 August 2025). Row 47 adds, from Anthropic's own Project Vend report, the hallucinated payment account and the restocking contact who did not exist. Row 56 adds the Attorney General's allegations at paras 57 and 60, and its pinpoints follow. Row 63 adds, from the developer's own account, the rollback that took around 24 hours. Row 66 adds paras 2.59, 2.65 and 2.66 of the ANAO audit report (pp.37 to 39), and its pinpoints follow. Row 69 adds para 66 of the Wisconsin Supreme Court's decision in State v. Loomis. Each of these rows keeps its marker. The text of row 70 does not change.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4.5 <code>README.md</code></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The incident register row gives 101 publicly documented incidents, in place of 91. It labels row 96 as one observational study of a deployed practice with no harm event, beside the four automation or non-AI analogues and the one audit finding.</li>\n<li><strong>The version line, the banner and the errata record.</strong> The version line names this release, 1.3.4, in place of 1.3.3, and the banner gives its date, 22 October 2026, in place of 9 October 2026. The errata record names this correction log, <code>00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-10-22.md</code>, as \"v1.3.4, changes to text signed at v1.3.3\".</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"5. Canvas citations of incident rows, and other research-basis additions","html":"<p>Each citation of a register row below is in the reserved form. Each canvas's change log records its change. No ★ rating or risk-profile value changes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Citations restated after the new reading.</strong></li>\n<li>IRQ-HUM-009 field 18: the MiDAS limb (row 28) gives the figures of the agency's review and keeps the 93% as pleaded only. It records that the state's audit did not review the identification of fraudulent claims. The TOEIC limb (row 29) describes the classifications as software with review by the testing firm's own listeners, and gives the departures and caveats of the NAO.</li>\n<li>IRQ-CON-004 field 18: the NYC MyCity citation no longer says \"RAG grounding\". It says that the city presented the chatbot as drawing on its own business web pages. The two incidents are called chatbot mis-statements, not generative ones, because the decision in Moffatt records that Air Canada gave no information about the nature of its chatbot, and no source read for row 19 states the chatbot's architecture. The field cites the two incidents in the reserved form, as register rows 1 and 19, with the limits of each row. Row 1 is a small-claims decision of a tribunal, in aviation. Row 19 rests on a newsroom's own testing and records no ruling, so the field no longer says that both incidents crystallised as liability.</li>\n<li>IRQ-DAT-014 field 18 (row 64): the study is read in full, and the row stays secondary.</li>\n<li>IRQ-HUM-002 field 18 (row 71): the defendant's filed answer is added. It denies the paragraph 3 allegations and that PxDx is an algorithm.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-014 field 18 (row 86), IRQ-AGT-004 field 18 (row 50) and IRQ-SEC-005 field 18 (row 80): each records the vendor's later document in place of \"no statement from the vendor was read\". Each row stays secondary.</li>\n<li><strong>Citations of rows 92 to 101.</strong> IRQ-SEC-005 (row 92). IRQ-AGT-003 and IRQ-SEC-011 (row 94). IRQ-DAT-009 (row 95). IRQ-HUM-003 (row 96). IRQ-HUM-008 (row 97). IRQ-MOD-007 and IRQ-MOD-008 (rows 98 and 99). IRQ-MOD-009 (row 99). IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-011 (row 100). IRQ-CON-009 (row 101).</li>\n<li>IRQ-CON-009 field 18 cites row 101 as a financial-services instance of harmful output to a customer in a service exchange, carried as a secondary row. The press report gives only the last turns, and the bank's statement refers to the language that the assistant was exposed to. So the row does not show that the use was wholly non-adversarial, and the field's [UNVERIFIED] record of no such financial-services incident stands.</li>\n<li><strong>Citations of rows already in the register.</strong> IRQ-AGT-003 (row 17). IRQ-AGT-010 (row 47). IRQ-GOV-004 and IRQ-TPR-003 (row 66, an audit finding of control gaps, not a harm event). IRQ-GOV-008 (row 2, a pre-AI automation analogue); its analogue sentence now says that one such finding is cited. IRQ-GOV-011 (row 56, allegations that the firm denies). IRQ-HUM-007 (row 69). IRQ-HUM-008 (row 70). IRQ-RES-004 field 18 (row 63). IRQ-MOD-016 cites row 63 in field 10, as a worked example of expected evidence; its research-basis date does not move.</li>\n<li><strong>Research basis without a register row.</strong> These two citations are not in the reserved form.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-006 field 18 cites Anthropic, \"How we built our multi-agent research system\" (13 June 2025). Its early agents spawned 50 subagents for simple queries, and its limit was written as scaling rules in the prompts. The canvas reads the post as a record of behaviour found and fixed before release, on the way from prototype to production, so it cites the post and not a register row.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-013 field 18 cites Anthropic, \"Developing a computer use model\" (22 October 2024). During demonstrations for the public beta, the model clicked to stop a screen recording, and the footage was lost. Its view of the screen can miss short-lived actions or notifications. The post records no harm beyond the lost footage, so the canvas cites the post and not a register row.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"6. Under-research observations","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>URO-7 and URO-8 at their signing-batch limit.</strong> This release is a signing batch under <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2: it adds incident register rows 92 to 101 and one entry to the regulatory source register. URO-7 (IRQ-SEC-003) and URO-8 (IRQ-SEC-001) therefore passed their signing-batch limit unresolved. Each stays open to its date bound of 8 April 2027, and the ★4 of each canvas stands. A note in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> records this.</li>\n</ul>"}]};
